const ANSWER_KEY = {"1": "C", "2": "B", "3": "A", "4": "D", "5": "D", "6": "C", "7": "A", "8": "E", "9": "C", "10": "H", "11": "C", "12": "A", "13": "E", "14": "D", "15": "C", "16": "E", "17": "C", "18": "A", "19": "D", "20": "D", "21": "A", "22": "E", "23": "D", "24": "B", "25": "D", "26": "A", "27": "A", "28": "E", "29": "C", "30": "A", "31": "C", "32": "D", "33": "A", "34": "C", "35": "D", "36": "G", "37": "B", "38": "B", "39": "D", "40": "A", "41": "C", "42": "E", "43": "A", "44": "E", "45": "D", "46": "E", "47": "D", "48": "A", "49": "E", "50": "E"};
const EXPLANATIONS = {"1": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Oral contraceptives are contraindicated in patients who are age 35 years or older and smoke at least 15 cigarettes daily and in patients with increased risk for cardiovascular events (eg, severe hypertension, ischemic heart disease, valvular disease, diabetes mellitus with end-organ damage), history of stroke or other thromboembolism, cirrhosis, migraine with aura, and breast malignancy with estrogen or progesterone receptor positivity.</p><p>Many methods of contraception exist, including temporary and permanent options, hormonal and nonhormonal devices, oral medications, injections, implants, and ovulation tracking. The most appropriate contraceptive method is dependent on the patient and her goals of care. Oral contraceptives (OCPs) are a temporary form of birth control that must be taken every day. They commonly include combinations of ethinyl estradiol and progestin, although progestin-only options are available. OCPs work to prevent pregnancy by thickening the cervical mucus, thinning the endometrial lining, and decreasing ovulation. While effective in preventing pregnancy when taken as instructed, there are important contraindications to prevent cardiovascular morbidity and venous thromboembolism. Pertinent to this patient, OCPs are contraindicated in women who are age 35 years or older and smoke at least 15 cigarettes daily. They are also contraindicated in patients with increased risk for cardiovascular events (eg, severe hypertension, ischemic heart disease, valvular disease, diabetes mellitus with end-organ damage), history of stroke or other thromboembolism, cirrhosis, migraine with aura, and breast malignancy with estrogen or progesterone receptor positivity.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Diaphragm (Choice A) is a barrier contraceptive that fits on top of the cervix and anterior wall of the vagina. It must be placed prior to sexual intercourse and left in place for 6 to 8 hours after use. It is typically used with spermicide. Although this patient has no contraindications to diaphragm use, it is less effective at preventing pregnancy than some of the other options. IUD (Choice B) is a long-active reversible contraceptive option that does not require active compliance on the part of the patient. It is a reliable form of contraception with failure rates as low as or lower than 1% and may last up to 10 years. Contraindications include an active pelvic infection or structural abnormality of the uterus, neither of which are present in this patient. Spermicidal foam (Choice D) is a foam used during sexual intercourse that is placed in the vagina and designed to kill sperm. It is approximately 70% effective at preventing pregnancy with typical use. This patient has no contraindications to its use. Tubal ligation (Choice E) refers to the surgical procedure of dividing the bilateral fallopian tubes as a method of contraception. Pregnancy is prevented as ovulated eggs cannot travel through the fallopian tube to the uterus, where they may be fertilized and implanted. It is a permanent method of contraception and may be an appropriate choice for this couple. Vasectomy (Choice F) refers to the ligation of the vas deferens to prevent sperm from reaching the ejaculate and subsequently causing pregnancy. This is a permanent form of sterilization and may be an appropriate choice for this couple.</p>", "2": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Rh(D)-negative mothers are at a high risk for Rh(D) alloimmunization when exposed to Rh(D) antigens from the fetal circulation. Alloimmunization can be prevented by the administration of Rh(D) immune globulin at 28 weeks of gestation, along with following the delivery of an Rh(D)-positive newborn, any invasive procedure (eg, amniocentesis), and any episodes of fetomaternal bleeding. Administration of Rh(D) immune globulin is ineffective in a patient who has already developed Rh(D) antibodies.</p><p>Rh(D)-negative mothers are at a high risk for developing anti-Rh(D) antibodies if exposed to blood from a Rh(D)-positive fetus, which most commonly occurs during delivery, although it can occur during prepartum bleeding or invasive procedures, such as amniocentesis or chorionic villus sampling. If Rh(D) antigens from the fetal circulation enter the maternal circulation, it is possible that the mother’s immune system will form antibodies against the Rh(D) antigen. While this carries no significant complication with the initial exposure, a subsequent pregnancy with an Rh(D)-positive fetus can result in severe hemolytic anemia of the newborn (erythroblastosis fetalis) as maternal antibodies cause hemolysis of fetal erythrocytes when they cross the placenta. Rh(D) immune globulin (RhoGAM) acts by binding to Rh(D)-positive cells that enter the maternal circulation, preventing the immune system from developing antibodies to the Rh(D) antigen. Administration of Rh(D) immune globulin is typically performed for Rh(D)-negative mothers at 28 weeks of gestation, along with following the delivery of an Rh(D)-positive newborn, any invasive procedure (eg, amniocentesis or chorionic villus sampling), and any episodes of fetomaternal bleeding. Rh(D) immune globulin is ineffective in a patient who has already developed Rh(D) antibodies. Thus, if this patient's antibody screening is negative, she should receive Rh(D) immune globulin at her 28 weeks’ gestation visit.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Administration of the measles-mumps-rubella vaccine at this visit (Choice A) is inappropriate in a pregnant patient, as it is a live attenuated virus. Live vaccines are contraindicated in pregnant patients as they can lead to infection of the fetus. Rubella infection of an unvaccinated pregnant patient can lead to fetal defects such as sensorineural deafness, neurodevelopmental delay, congenital cardiac defects, microcephaly. and extramedullary hematopoiesis. Therefore, this patient should be vaccinated after delivery. Anovaginal culture for Streptococcus agalactiae (group B) at 36 weeks’ gestation (Choice C) is routine. However, it is not necessary in this patient as she has already tested positive for S. agalactiae in her urine earlier in this pregnancy. Thus, she should receive prophylactic antibiotic therapy during delivery to prevent fetal infection. Fluorescent treponemal antibody assay at 28 weeks’ gestation (Choice D) is not recommended in asymptomatic patients, especially in those with minimal risk factors (eg, multiple partners, lack of barrier protection) who had a negative screen at their initial prenatal appointment. Primary syphilis most commonly presents with a nonpainful genital lesion, while secondary syphilis presents with a maculopapular rash and condyloma lata. Infection in pregnant patients can lead to neonatal abnormalities, such as hepatomegaly, rhinitis, maculopapular rash, epitrochlear lymphadenopathy, and chorioretinitis. Repeat urine culture now and during the third trimester (Choice E) is not necessary. While bacteriuria and urinary tract infections can lead to obstetric complications such as preterm labor, screening for asymptomatic bacteriuria is recommended only once in early pregnancy. This patient has received appropriate antibiotic therapy for her asymptomatic bacteriuria. Repeat testing with urine culture should only be done for symptomatic urethritis, cystitis, or pyelonephritis.</p>", "3": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Urethral carcinoma is a rare pelvic malignancy that is associated with increased age, chronic inflammation, and human papillomavirus. It is more common in men than in women. A mass near the urethral opening may be present on examination, and the patient may show signs of overflow incontinence given the physical obstruction of the mass. It is treated with surgical resection, as well as chemotherapy and radiation therapy depending on the stage.</p><p>This patient with symptoms of overflow urinary incontinence (unprecipitated urine leakage, inability to empty the bladder) secondary to obstruction from a large, friable mass near the urethral opening most likely has urethral carcinoma. Overall, this is a rare cancer that is more common in men than in women. In women, it most commonly presents with lower urinary tract symptoms, such as dysuria or dyspareunia. Evaluation should include a full physical examination, including a bimanual examination, laboratory studies to rule out a urinary tract infection, and further imaging, such as CT scan/MRI or cystourethroscopy. The most common type of urethral carcinoma in women is squamous cell carcinoma, and treatment includes resection, as well as chemotherapy or radiation therapy depending on the extent of disease. Risk factors for its development include age, the presence of chronic inflammation or a urethral diverticulum, and human papillomavirus infection.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. 4 Urethral diverticulum (Choice B) is an outpouching of the urethra that presents with recurrent urinary tract infections, dysuria, dyspareunia, and postvoid dribbling. It would not cause the large external pelvic mass seen in this patient. Urethritis secondary to estrogen deficiency (Choice C) most commonly occurs in postmenopausal women with urgency, frequency, and urge or stress incontinence. The lack of estrogen associated with menopause leads to atrophy and inflammation of the vagina and urethra, which can lead to pelvic floor laxity and symptoms of an overactive bladder. This patient has overflow incontinence, rather than urge incontinence, caused by the physical obstruction of her urethra by the tumor. Urethrocele (Choice D) is caused by prolapse of the urethra into the anterior wall of the vagina, and is a common cause of stress urinary incontinence, especially in multiparous women. It is differentiated from other forms of stress incontinence by urethra hypermobility. This patient's symptoms are more likely secondary to the obstructive effect of a urethral carcinoma. Urethrovaginal fistula (Choice E) occurs when a tract opens between the urethra and the vaginal canal, which results in painless urine leakage from the vagina. It may be otherwise asymptomatic or present with recurrent urinary tract infections. It is most commonly caused by gynecologic surgery or radiation therapy, neither of which this patient has had. Additionally, she has overflow incontinence rather than a constant flow of urine leakage, making urethral carcinoma more likely than a urethrovaginal fistula.</p>", "4": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: A pulmonary embolus (PE) presents with acute-onset dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, tachypnea, and hypoxia. Gravid and postpartum patients are at increased risk for deep venous thrombosis and PE due to hypercoagulability and venous stasis. For postpartum patients, diagnosis is confirmed with spiral CT angiography of the chest. For gravid patients, nuclear medicine ventilation-perfusion scans of the lung are preferred because of the lower amount of fetal radiation exposure.</p><p>This patient's acute-onset dyspnea and chest pain, tachycardia, tachypnea, and hypoxia are highly suggestive of a pulmonary embolus (PE). A minority of patients with PE present with a mild fever, which can create diagnostic confusion. ECG commonly discloses sinus tachycardia without ST segment depression or elevation. Pregnancy represents a hypercoagulable state, during which coagulation factors |, Il, VII, VIII, IX, and X are overexpressed, while the anticoagulant protein S is underexpressed. Similarly, hormonal changes and compression by the gravid uterus lead to venous stasis and pooling of blood in the lower extremities and pelvis, common sites of venous thrombosis. Gravid patients are therefore at increased risk for deep venous thrombosis (DVT) and subsequent PE throughout pregnancy and in the postpartum period. Further evaluation should be performed to confirm the diagnosis, preferably with spiral CT angiography of the chest. For gravid patients, nuclear medicine ventilation- perfusion scans of the lung are preferred because of the lower amount of fetal radiation exposure.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E. Abdominal ultrasonography (Choice A) is useful in the diagnosis of acute abdominal pain in pregnant and postpartum patients. Conditions that can be diagnosed with this modality include biliary pathologies (eg, cholelithiasis, cholecystitis, choledocholithiasis) and gastrointestinal tract pathologies (eg, appendicitis). This patient's presentation is highly suspicious for a pulmonary pathology, and imaging of the chest must be performed. Doppler ultrasonography of the lower extremities (Choice B) is useful to confirm the diagnosis of a suspected DVT. DVT presents with unilateral lower extremity pain, erythema, and edema, along with tenderness on palpation and pain exacerbated with passive stretch of the foot. While PE is often caused by DVT, searching for a DVT will not aid in confirming suspicion of PE and will delay diagnosis and treatment. Echocardiography (Choice C) is useful in the evaluation of cardiac pathologies such as myocardial infarction and heart failure. Cardiopulmonary examination in these patients may disclose abnormal rhythm, abnormal heart sounds, or evidence of fluid overload (eg, crackles on pulmonary auscultation). ECG will disclose ST depressions or elevations in myocardial infarction. Echocardiography is indicated in the further evaluation of PE if right heart strain is suspected, but spiral CT angiography to confirm the diagnosis of PE should be performed first. MRI of the abdomen (Choice E) can be performed in the evaluation of postpartum abdominal pain if ultrasonography or CT scan is nondiagnostic. This patient's abdomen is appropriately tender, and her pulmonary findings require prompt evaluation with imaging of the chest.</p>", "5": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Group B streptococcus (GBS) is a common cause of asymptomatic bacteriuria and other infections, such as cystitis, pyelonephritis, chorioamnionitis, and endometritis in pregnant patients. As maternal colonization and/or infection is associated with increased risk for neonatal transmission and consequent neonatal sepsis, current recommendations include a urine culture early in pregnancy, as well as screening at 36 to 38 weeks’ gestation. Those with a positive culture or a symptomatic GBS infection during pregnancy should be treated with intrapartum antibiotics to decrease neonatal transmission.</p><p>Aurinary tract infection often presents with dysuria, frequency, urgency, and suprapubic discomfort, most commonly because of ascending migration of enteric bacteria through the urethra (eg, Escherichia coli). When untreated, this can ascend farther and cause pyelonephritis, or bacterial infection of the kidneys. This is characterized by fever, leukocytosis, nausea, vomiting, and costovertebral angle tenderness. While group B streptococcus (GBS) frequently colonizes the urethra, vagina, and rectum, it rarely causes infection in immunocompetent adults. In pregnancy, however, infection with GBS is not uncommon. Pregnant patients can develop asymptomatic bacteriuria, cystitis, pyelonephritis, chorioamnionitis, and endometritis linked to GBS. Maternal colonization and infection increase the risk for neonatal transmission. Therefore, pregnant patients are screened with a urine culture early in pregnancy and an anovaginal swab at 36 to 38 weeks’ gestation. If either of these are positive or the patient develops a GBS infection during pregnancy, they should be treated with intrapartum antibiotic therapy during labor to prevent neonatal transmission. This is important as GBS is a leading cause of neonatal sepsis, which is associated with significant morbidity and mortality.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E. Screening for group B streptococcal infection at 35 weeks’ gestation (Choice A) or screening for group B streptococcal infection at the onset of labor (Choice B) is not indicated, as this patient currently has a known GBS infection. Screening would likely be positive because of her infection during pregnancy; this patient should be treated with intrapartum antibiotic therapy regardless of the result. common. Because of her infection during pregnancy, this patient should be treated with intrapartum antibiotic therapy to prevent neonatal transmission regardless of a screening result. Maternal antibiotic therapy at 35 weeks’ gestation (Choice C) would be ineffective in preventing neonatal transmission of GBS. Neonates are at increased risk for acquiring GBS, which can lead to neonatal sepsis, during delivery. Therefore, this patient should be treated with antibiotic therapy once she goes into labor and not before. Elective cesarean delivery at 38 weeks’ gestation (Choice E) is not required for a history of GBS infection. While this would decrease the risk for neonatal transmission as the fetus would not be exposed to GBS in the vagina, early elective cesarean delivery is often associated with more complications than a spontaneous vaginal delivery.</p>", "6": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Endometriosis presents with chronic dyspareunia, dysmenorrhea, and dyschezia caused by the presence of ectopic endometrial tissue outside the uterus. The most common complication is infertility.</p><p>Infertility is the most common complication of endometriosis, or the presence of ectopic endometrial tissue outside the uterus. This ectopic tissue may occur close to the uterus, such as on the cervix, within a fallopian tube, on the broad ligament, or on an ovary. It may also be distant, such as on the visceral peritoneal surface of the intestines or rectum, within the peritoneal cavity, or within the thorax. Endometrial tissue remains hormonally sensitive, and symptoms often fluctuate on a cyclic, monthly basis as the tissue progresses through the histologic phases of proliferative and secretory endometrium, then sloughs during menses. Symptoms include chronic dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and dyschezia. Pelvic examination will often show tenderness of the uterine wall and surrounding structures with possible uterine immobility secondary to pelvic adhesions. Endometrial implants on the cervix or vaginal wall may also be seen. Infertility may occur as a result of tubal obstruction or architectural changes from chronic inflammation and scarring. The diagnosis of endometriosis is definitively made through laparoscopy to visualize and sample endometriotic implants.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. Endometrial cancer (Choice A) typically presents with postmenopausal uterine bleeding, although it can also present with abnormal uterine bleeding in a premenopausal […] Endometritis (Choice B) is an acute bacterial infection of the endometrium. Chlamydia trachomatis is a common cause in young, sexually active women. Symptoms include fever, chills, nausea, abdominal pain, uterine bleeding, foul-smelling discharge, and cervical and uterine tenderness on examination. Patients with endometriosis are not at increased risk for developing endometritis. Leiomyosarcoma (Choice D) is a rare malignancy of the uterine myometrium. It presents with abnormal uterine bleeding, pelvic pain, and bloating. A pelvic mass may be evident on physical examination or imaging. Risk factors include radiation to the pelvis, tamoxifen therapy, and familial conditions. Endometriosis is not a risk factor. Recurrent abortion (Choice E) refers to the recurrent loss of a fetus before 20 weeks’ gestation, which most commonly presents with acute vaginal bleeding and cramping. Risk factors include age greater than 35 years, prior fetal demise, uterine anatomic distortion, diabetes, thyroid disorders, coagulopathies or hypercoagulability, and substance abuse. While endometriosis can cause infertility, it less commonly causes spontaneous abortion.</p>", "7": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Breast cancer is the most common nondermatologic malignancy in women. It may cause a palpable breast mass, be found on routine screening mammography, or cause symptoms of metastatic disease. A common site of metastasis is bone, where it presents with a mixed lytic and blastic lesion, rest or nighttime pain, weight loss, and pathologic fractures. Patients with advanced, metastatic breast cancer are typically treated with palliative systemic therapy.</p><p>Breast cancer is the most common nondermatologic malignancy in women, and commonly presents with a breast mass, skin or nipple retraction, or axillary lymphadenopathy. However, it may be asymptomatic and diagnosed on screening mammography. More rarely, it may be found because of symptoms of metastasis, as in this patient. The most common sites of metastasis are bone, liver, lungs, and brain. When breast cancer metastasizes to bone, it causes mixed lytic and blastic lesions that can lead to bone pain and pathologic fractures. Red flag symptoms for metastatic bone disease include rest pain or pain that awakens the patient at night, in addition to unintentional weight loss. Risk factors for breast cancer development include exogenous estrogen administration, a family history of premenopausal breast cancer, genetic mutations (eg, BRCA1 and BRCA2), early menarche, late menopause, nulliparity, obesity, and African American ethnicity. A family history of multiple first-degree relatives, particularly when the relatives are premenopausal at diagnosis, greatly increases the risk. In patients with early-stage breast cancer, treatment consists of surgical removal of the tumor and axillary lymph nodes along with adjuvant systemic hormonal therapy, biologic therapy, or chemotherapy, which depends on the estrogen, progesterone, and HER2 receptor positivity of the cancer. Patients with advanced breast cancer are typically treated with life-prolonging, palliative systemic therapy only.</p><p>[…] Endometrium (Choice C) cancer is the most common gynecologic cancer in the United States and most classically presents with postmenopausal uterine bleeding. Risk factors include chronically increased estrogen concentrations, unopposed estrogen therapy, long-term anovulation, obesity, estrogen-secreting tumors, and endometrial hyperplasia. Endometrial cancer metastasizes locally to the bladder or rectum, but not to the bones. Ovarian (Choice D) cancer typically presents indolently with abdominal distension and weight loss in a postmenopausal woman. Risk factors include a family history of ovarian cancer, genetic mutation (BRCA), increased age, smoking, nulliparity, and polycystic ovarian syndrome. Ovarian cancer is a rare cause of bony metastasis. Stomach (Choice E) cancer most commonly presents with weight loss, epigastric abdominal pain, nausea, and, occasionally, gastrointestinal bleeding. Areas of metastasis include the liver and lymph nodes, and less commonly the brain or lungs. While bony metastasis can occur, gastric cancer is a less common cause than breast cancer.</p>", "8": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Bacterial vaginosis is caused by the vaginal overgrowth of Gardnerella vaginalis, a gram-variable, facultative anaerobe. It presents with gray, thin, malodorous vaginal discharge, a vaginal pH greater than 4.5, and a fishy odor on KOH testing. Identification of clue cells on wet mount microscopy is a useful confirmatory test.</p><p>Bacterial vaginosis is a common gynecologic condition characterized by a shift of vaginal flora and overgrowth of bacterial species, commonly Gardnerella vaginalis. G. vaginalis is a gram-variable, facultative anaerobe. Bacterial vaginosis presents with gray, thin, malodorous vaginal discharge. Vaginal pH is increased greater than 4.5, and a fishy odor is detected on KOH testing. The identification of clue cells on wet mount preparation is the most appropriate confirmatory test. Metronidazole is the first-line therapy and may be delivered orally or intravaginally. Bacterial vaginosis is not considered a sexually transmitted infection, and treatment of asymptomatic sexual partners is not recommended.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b>: Diluted vinegar douche (Choice A) is inappropriate. Douching refers to the process of cleaning the vagina with liquid. While previously thought to remove pathogens and prevent infections, it has been shown to increase the risk for infection and chemical vaginitis. Additionally, it would not treat this patient's underlying infection. Intramuscular ceftriaxone and oral doxycycline for the patient and oral doxycycline for her sexual partners (Choice B) is used in the treatment of Neisseria gonorrhoeae (ceftriaxone) and Chlamydia trachomatis (doxycycline). These sexually transmitted infections often present with urethritis, dysuria, and cervicitis. Gonorrhea classically presents with mucopurulent discharge, whereas the discharge of chlamydia is more commonly thin and watery. These infections do not present with clue cells on microscopic examination. Oral fluconazole for the patient only (Choice C) is used in the treatment of vulvovaginal candidiasis, which presents with vaginal pruritus, vulvar erythema, and a thick, white vaginal discharge. Candida as a cause of vaginal discharge can be distinguished from other potential causes by its normal vaginal pH, negative KOH testing, and absence of motile organisms on wet mount preparation. Microscopic examination will show pseudohyphae rather than clue cells. Oral metronidazole for the patient and her sexual partners (Choice D) is not necessary. While oral metronidazole would be appropriate treatment for this patient's bacterial vaginosis, treatment of her partners is not necessary, as bacterial vaginosis is not considered a sexually transmitted infection.</p>", "9": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Placenta previa refers to the abnormal positioning of placental tissue such that the placenta partially or completely overlies the internal cervical os. It most commonly presents with painless third trimester vaginal bleeding and frequently is diagnosed on routine prenatal ultrasonography. Risk factors for placenta previa include causes of uterine scarring, such as multiparity, previous cesarean delivery, and prior dilatation and curettage.</p><p>Placenta previa refers to the abnormal positioning of placental tissue such that the placenta partially or completely overlies the internal cervical os. It most commonly presents with painless third trimester vaginal bleeding. Frequently, it is detected on prenatal ultrasonography, but it may not always be evident. Thus, patients who present with vaginal bleeding after 20 weeks’ gestation should not undergo a digital cervical examination until ultrasonography has been performed to determine the exact positioning of the placenta, because severe hemorrhage can occur if a placenta previa is present. Risk factors for placenta previa include causes of uterine scarring, such as multiparity, previous cesarean delivery, and prior dilatation and curettage. While it may self-resolve as pregnancy progresses, its presence in the late third trimester requires careful management. Treatment of an initial bleed without significant hemodynamic instability necessitates intravenous administration of fluids and observation. Patients who are able to be stabilized may be expectantly managed with a plan for a cesarean delivery around 37 weeks’ gestation, to allow time for sufficient fetal development. A cesarean delivery is indicated to prevent significant hemorrhage, maternal/fetal complications, and potential fetal demise.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Immediate cesarean delivery is […] Abruptio placentae (Choice A) presents with vaginal bleeding, severe uterine pain, and tetanic contractions, typically in the third trimester, because of premature separation of the placenta from the uterus. It commonly results from preeclampsia, lower abdominal trauma, smoking, or cocaine use. This patient has painless vaginal bleeding without contractions on tocometry, making placenta previa the most likely diagnosis. Cervical cancer (Choice B) is frequently asymptomatic but can present with postcoital vaginal bleeding. Most commonly, it is detected on routine Pap smear and is associated with human papillomavirus. This patient is up-to-date on her cervical cancer screening and had vaginal bleeding that began 2 days after sexual intercourse, making cervical cancer a less likely diagnosis. Premature contractions (Choice D) are uterine contractions that occur after 20 and prior to 37 weeks’ gestation. If the contractions lead to cervical change, it is referred to as preterm labor. This patient's tocometry shows no contractions, making this answer choice incorrect. Vaginal laceration (Choice E) is a common cause of postpartum hemorrhage. It can also occur after sexual trauma or rarely during consensual intercourse. This patient has no reported history of sexual trauma and had sexual intercourse 2 days ago. Vaginal laceration is thus a less likely cause of her persistent vaginal bleeding.</p>", "10": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Vesicovaginal fistulas can be a consequence of gynecologic surgery, pelvic pathology, or radiation therapy. They typically present with painless urinary leakage from the bladder into the vagina. Treatment includes surgical repair.</p><p>A vesicovaginal fistula can be a consequence of gynecologic surgery, pelvic pathology, or radiation therapy. It occurs when a tract opens between the bladder and the vaginal canal and results in painless, usually asymptomatic urinary leakage from the vagina that is often worse with standing. Less commonly, symptoms may include fever if the fistula develops as a result of infection or inflammation or if an infection is a result of the fistula. Genitourinary examination will show a pool of urine in the vaginal canal, but the fistula site may be too small or difficult to visualize. Confirmatory testing entails infusing dyed sterile fluid into the bladder, then evaluating the vagina for the presence of transmitted dye. Complications include an increased risk for cystitis and ascending urinary tract infection caused by the transmission of vaginal flora. Treatment of a vesicovaginal fistula involves surgical repair and antibiotics to prevent or treat infection.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, E, F, and G. Cystitis (Choice A), or inflammation of the bladder, is commonly caused by an ascending infection from the urethra. Symptoms include dysuria, urinary urgency, urinary frequency, and suprapubic discomfort. While this patient is at increased risk for developing cystitis, she has no symptoms to suggest its presence currently. Cystocele (Choice B) is common in multiparous patients due to weakness of the pelvic floor, which causes the bladder to prolapse into the anterior vaginal wall. It is associated with stress urinary incontinence, or loss of urine with an increase in abdominal pressure (eg, coughing or sneezing), which this patient does not have. Rectovaginal fistula (Choice C) refers to a fistula formed between the rectum and vagina. It presents with foul-smelling vaginal discharge, passage of gas from the vagina, and vaginal irritation. It may predispose the patient to urinary tract infections. This patient has watery discharge, rather than the loss of gas or foul-smelling discharge, which makes a vesicovaginal fistula more likely. Urethral prolapse (Choice D) and urethrocele (Choice F) occur when the urethra prolapses through the external urethral opening itself or into the anterior vaginal wall, respectively, due to the loss of proximal support, most commonly in a multiparous patient. They present with stress incontinence, and the urethra will be visible at the external urethral opening or palpable on the vaginal wall on physical examination. Neither would cause watery vaginal discharge. Urethritis (Choice E) presents with similar symptoms to cystitis, such as dysuria, urinary frequency, and urinary urgency. It is commonly caused by enteric bacteria. This patient has no symptoms of urethritis. Vaginal vault prolapse (Choice G) occurs secondary to pelvic floor dysfunction and weakness, most commonly in multiparous patients. This weakness causes herniation of the vaginal vault through the vagina and vaginal opening and presents with a heavy sensation in the pelvic area, urinary incontinence, and constipation or difficulty initiating a bowel movement. It would not present with painless, clear vaginal discharge.</p>", "11": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Herpes genitalis is characterized by painful vesicles and ulcers on an erythematous base. Symptoms typically include local pain and itching, dysuria, and tender lymphadenopathy. Treatment includes acyclovir or valacyclovir.</p><p>Herpes simplex virus (HSV) genital infection is a sexually transmitted infection that is characterized by painful vesicles in the genital area (eg, vulva, vagina, cervix, perineum). Most cases of genital herpes are caused by HSV-2; however, HSV-1 has also been associated with genital herpes infection. Herpes genitalis typically begins with pain and paresthesia, followed by the formation of vesicles, which lyse and progress to shallow, round, painful ulcers with an erythematous border. This painful rash can be exacerbated during urination, in which acidic urine passes over open vesicles or ulcers. The erosions are also frequently accompanied by fever, chills, and inguinal lymphadenopathy. HSV is treated with acyclovir or valacyclovir, which decrease the duration of viral shedding and symptoms.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, E, and F. Chancroid (Choice A) is caused by infection with Haemophilus ducreyi. It commonly begins with an erythematous papule that progresses to a pustule, and finally, to a painful ulcer. The ulcer is typically exudative and accompanied by significant inguinal lymphadenopathy. In contrast to herpes genitalis, it more commonly causes a single lesion rather than multiple small lesions. 4 be asymptomatic. Complications include cervicitis, urethritis, epididymitis, and pelvic inflammatory disease. It does not cause painful genital ulcers. Chlamydia trachomatis infection (Choice B) is a sexually transmitted infection that classically presents with urethritis, cervicitis, or pelvic inflammatory disease and is frequently asymptomatic. Complications include cervicitis, urethritis, epididymitis, and pelvic inflammatory disease. Lymphogranuloma venereum (Choice D) is caused by Chlamydia trachomatis subtypes L1, L2, and L3. Primary infection presents with small, nonpainful genital ulcers that are self-limited. In contrast, secondary infection presents with severe inguinal lymphadenopathy (eg, buboes) and systemic symptoms, such as fever. It does not cause painful genital vesicles and ulcers. Molluscum contagiosum (Choice E), caused by poxvirus, is characterized by small painless papules with a central umbilication. They may appear anywhere on the body and are treated with cryotherapy or topical trichloroacetic acid and benzoyl peroxide. Molluscum contagiosum would not be expected to cause painful genital lesions. Trichomoniasis (Choice F) is a sexually transmitted infection that causes vaginitis and cervicitis in women, and urethritis in men. It is caused by trichomonas vaginalis, an anaerobic, flagellated protozoan. Trichomoniasis presents with pruritus, dyspareunia, cervical erythema, and abnormal discharge that is described as green, frothy, and malodorous. It does not cause genital lesions.</p>", "12": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: In pregnant patients or patients who have just undergone delivery, increased intra-abdominal pressure caused by the gravid uterus pushes on the diaphragm and intrathoracic cavity, decreasing the ability for the lungs to expand normally. This increases the risk for atelectasis, which can cause a mild fever within 2 days after delivery.</p><p>Atelectasis is a common cause of mild fever in the first 2 days after delivery or surgery. The term refers to the partial or complete collapse or incomplete expansion of pulmonary parenchyma. There are multiple types of atelectasis, including obstructive, passive, compressive, cicatricial, and adhesive. It is most common in patients who are pregnant, immobile, or in the postsurgical state, who are unable to produce adequate tidal volume to expand the lungs. In pregnant patients or patients who have just undergone vaginal delivery, increased intra-abdominal pressure caused by the gravid uterus pushes on the diaphragm and intrathoracic cavity, decreasing the ability for the lungs to expand normally. Physical examination will show diminished respiratory effort, often with decreased breath sounds and dullness to percussion at the lung bases corresponding to the regions of nonaerated, collapsed lung and inadequate diaphragmatic excursion. Mild hypoxia may be present. Pain control, incentive spirometry, and respiratory therapy with lung expansion maneuvers are key initial steps in management.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. Bacterial pneumonia (Choice B) is characterized by fever, dyspnea, a cough productive of sputum, malaise, and chest discomfort, most often 2 or more days after delivery or surgery. The risk is increased for these patients due to respiratory splinting secondary to pain, with consequent atelectasis in addition to an impaired ability to clear secretions and prevent aspiration. On physical examination, there may be abnormal lung sounds such as crackles or rhonchi, along with increased tactile fremitus. This patient does not have a cough or any characteristic breath sounds. Endomyometritis (Choice C) is an acute, typically polymicrobial, infection of the uterine endometrium and myometrium by normal vaginal flora. Patients typically present with fever, significant tenderness of the uterine fundus, and mucopurulent vaginal discharge. A risk factor for the development of endomyometritis is prolonged rupture of membranes (&gt;18 hours) and would be unlikely in this patient. Pelvic abscess (Choice D) can occur as a delayed complication of chorioamnionitis, a polymicrobial infection of the fetal membranes that presents intrapartum, most commonly after prelabor or prolonged rupture of membranes due to the migration of vaginal flora into the uterus. An abscess would present as abdominal pain and tenderness, fever, nausea, and vomiting that persist despite adequate antibiotic treatment (due to failed antibiotic penetration into the abscess cavity). Management involves percutaneous needle aspiration and drainage. Septic pelvic thrombophlebitis (Choice E) is a rare sequela of endometritis that can lead to local invasion and infection of the pelvic vein walls, which causes intimal damage and thrombosis. This condition typically presents with pelvic pain and fever, which is often widely variable in course, ranging from normal temperature to extremely high. Diagnosis typically occurs through blood cultures. If this patient were persistently febrile despite appropriate antibiotic treatment for endometritis and puerperal sepsis, septic pelvic thrombophlebitis should be considered.</p>", "13": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: A normal intrauterine pregnancy often presents with amenorrhea, nausea, breast tenderness, fatigue, and, occasionally, vaginal bleeding. Formal diagnosis is made with urine or serum β-hCG, and ultrasonography can be used to evaluate for an intrauterine gestational sac, which should be present by 5 weeks of gestation.</p><p>Pregnancy is suspected when there is a missed or delayed menstrual period. Light vaginal bleeding can occur early in pregnancy, often caused by implantation of the embryo, which can occasionally be mistaken for menses. Other symptoms of early pregnancy include nausea, breast tenderness, and fatigue. Physical examination may show an enlarged, globular uterus, but formal diagnosis is made with urine or serum β-hCG. β-hCG is produced by the placenta, is undetectable in nonpregnant patients, and doubles approximately every 48 hours during an early normal pregnancy. In a normal pregnancy, an intrauterine gestational sac should be visible by 5 weeks gestational age. Additionally, transvaginal ultrasonography provides sufficient sensitivity to detect an intrauterine pregnancy once the β-hCG concentration exceeds 1000 to 2000 miU/mL, as in this patient. Given this patient's positive urine β-hCG testing, increased quantitative serum β-hCG concentration, and gestational sac on ultrasonography, the most likely diagnosis is a normal early intrauterine pregnancy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D. Abnormal intrauterine pregnancy (Choice A) is incorrect, as it is too early in this pregnancy to adequately evaluate the fetus. An abnormal intrauterine pregnancy most commonly refers to an empty gestational sac or a fetal pole without cardiac activity, neither of which is mentioned in this question stem. Thus, it is more likely at this point that the patient has a normal early intrauterine pregnancy. Ectopic pregnancy (Choice B) is an abnormal extrauterine pregnancy (eg, in the fallopian tube, cervix, ovary, or peritoneum) occurring in an anatomic location that cannot support the fetus. It commonly presents with vaginal bleeding, abdominal pain, and an abnormally low serum β-hCG, which does not follow the expected doubling every 48 hours. Additionally, ultrasonography would likely show the gestational sac outside of the uterus or no gestational sac at all in the event of an ectopic pregnancy. Hydatidiform mole (Choice C) presents with nausea, vomiting, vaginal bleeding, preeclampsia, and a uterine size greater than expected dates due to either fertilization of an empty ovum (complete mole, no fetal parts) or dual fertilization of a normal ovum (incomplete mole, fetal parts present). It is a nonviable pregnancy that is frequently described on ultrasonography as a “snowstorm” appearance. Laboratory studies may also show an abnormally increased β-hCG concentration, which is not found in this patient. Ovarian germ cell tumor (Choice D) includes teratomas, dysgerminomas, yolk sac tumors, and mixed germ cell tumors. Broadly, they present with abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding, and bloating, as well as increased concentrations of hormones such as β-hCG, alpha-fetoprotein, and lactate dehydrogenase. A gestational sac would not be present on ultrasonography; rather, a unilateral pelvic mass would be expected.</p>", "14": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Premature ovarian failure presents in reproductive-age women with anovulation, amenorrhea, and infertility, as well as signs of early menopause, such as night sweats, hot flashes, and vaginal atrophy. Laboratory studies show decreased estrogen with increased follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinizing hormone concentrations. Bleeding will not occur with a progesterone withdrawal test. It is commonly associated with other autoimmune disorders or karyotype abnormalities.</p><p>Premature ovarian failure, also known as autoimmune ovarian failure or primary ovarian insufficiency, classically presents in a woman of reproductive age with anovulation, amenorrhea, and infertility. It can also present with signs of early menopause, such as hot flashes, night sweats, and vaginal atrophy. It is caused by autoimmune inflammation and destruction within the ovaries, leading to a decrease in estrogen production, limited follicle reserve, and loss of ovarian stromal cells. Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinizing hormone (LH) concentrations are appropriately increased as a result of the lack of negative feedback on the anterior pituitary. The secondary amenorrhea may additionally be evaluated by a progesterone withdrawal test, as was done with this patient. This refers to a test in which the patient is prescribed a short course of progesterone and then is observed for uterine bleeding after its withdrawal. Progesterone therapy acts to inhibit FSH and LH, preventing ovulation, as well as to maintain the endometrial lining and cause its transition to a secretory, rather than proliferative, endometrium. When progesterone is withdrawn, bleeding occurs if the amenorrhea is caused by an abnormality or irregularity of the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis, such as anovulation. Bleeding will not occur if the abnormal bleeding is caused by a structural abnormality of the uterus or is in the setting of estrogen deficiency. Premature ovarian failure is most often idiopathic but is more common in women with karyotype abnormalities or other autoimmune diseases, such as chronic lymphocytic (Hashimoto) thyroiditis. h also present in premenopausal women with abnormal uterine bleeding. Risk factors include prolonged, unopposed exposure to estrogen, such as early menarche, late menopause, polycystic ovary syndrome, exogenous estrogen therapy, nulliparity, and obesity. This patient has amenorrhea rather than vaginal bleeding.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Hysteroscopy (Choice B) would be useful in evaluating the uterus for structural causes of amenorrhea, as it allows direct visualization of the uterine cavity. However, it is more commonly used in the diagnosis of polyps and fibroids, which present with irregular menstrual bleeding rather than amenorrhea. Measurement of serum estradiol-178 concentration (Choice C) would likely be low in this patient given her diminished ovarian reserve. This low concentration of serum estradiol is responsible for her symptoms of early menopause, such as night sweats and vaginal atrophy. However, this measurement would not establish the diagnosis of premature ovarian failure. Measurement of serum thyroid-stimulating hormone concentration (Choice E) could be used to evaluate the patient for hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism. Hypothyroidism presents with fatigue, weight gain, constipation, cold intolerance, bradycardia, dry and edematous skin, and delayed relaxation of deep tendon reflexes. In contrast, hyperthyroidism presents with heat intolerance, weight loss, palpitations, diaphoresis, and diarrhea. While both may cause menstrual irregularities, this patient has no other signs or symptoms to suggest one of these diseases.</p>", "15": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Secondary postpartum hemorrhage refers to bleeding that occurs 24 hours to 12 weeks after delivery. The most common causes include retained products of conception, infection, and bleeding from the placental bed. Retained products of conception are treated with dilatation and evacuation.</p><p>Postpartum hemorrhage (PPH) refers to blood loss associated with delivery exceeding 500 mL for a vaginal delivery and 1000 mL for a cesarean delivery. In the majority of cases, PPH will occur immediately following delivery (primary PPH). However, it can be delayed (secondary PPH), occurring over 24 hours or up to 12 weeks after delivery. The most common causes of primary PPH include uterine atony, vaginal or cervical laceration, and retained products of conception. The causes of secondary PPH include infection, bleeding from the placental attachment, and retained products of conception. While normal postpartum vaginal bleeding can persist for up to 8 weeks postpartum, secondary PPH is suggested when the bleeding soaks a menstrual pad in less than 1 hour or when the bleeding persists past 8 weeks postpartum. Retained products of conception as a cause of secondary PPH may also present with fever, abdominal pain, and an enlarged uterus. Ultrasonography will show echogenic material in the uterine cavity. Treatment includes dilatation and evacuation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. Cervical laceration (Choice A) is a frequent cause of PPH. It can occur during the normal course of childbirth and is repaired surgically. It more commonly causes bleeding immediately following delivery and would be a rare cause of delayed PPH. fundus, and mucopurulent vaginal discharge. This patient has no signs of infection, such as fever or uterine tenderness, making this a less likely cause of vaginal bleeding than retained products of conception. Endometritis (Choice B) is an acute, typically polymicrobial, infection of the uterine endometrium. It typically presents with fever and mucopurulent vaginal discharge. This patient has no signs of infection, such as fever or uterine tenderness, and her bleeding is more consistent with retained products of conception. Uterine atony (Choice D) is the most common cause of PPH immediately following delivery. It is diagnosed by a boggy, enlarged uterus on palpation. Risk factors include macrosomia, multiple gestations, prolonged labor, and uterine infection. It is not a cause of secondary PPH. Uterine inversion (Choice E) can occur during delivery of the placenta if too much traction is applied to the umbilical cord. This results in inversion of the uterine fundus into the endometrial cavity with severe cases presenting with the uterine fundus at the introitus. It is a rare but life-threatening cause of primary, not secondary, PPH.</p>", "16": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Yearly immunization against influenza is recommended for all patients and should be given ideally prior to the influenza season, or as soon as possible if the season is underway. Influenza vaccination is especially important for pregnant patients due to the increased risk for severe disease, hospitalization, morbidity, and mortality. Pregnant patients should receive the intramuscular inactivated version, rather than the intranasal live-attenuated version, due to the theoretical risk for transplacental transmission of live virus.</p><p>Influenza vaccines are redesigned each year to protect against a variety of different influenza strains. Thus, yearly influenza immunization is recommended for all patients and should be given ideally prior to the influenza season (generally October to April in the northern hemisphere), or as soon as possible if the season is already underway. The only contraindication to receiving the influenza vaccine is a prior severe allergic reaction to the vaccine or its components. Additionally, children younger than 6 months old should not receive the vaccine. Pregnancy commonly causes mild immunosuppression, increasing the risk for severe disease, hospitalization, and influenza-associated morbidity and mortality. Thus, prompt vaccination against influenza is especially important in pregnant patients. The influenza vaccine comes in two forms: an intramuscular inactivated virus vaccine and an intranasal live-attenuated virus vaccine. Pregnant patients should receive the inactivated version as the live-attenuated version carries the theoretical risk for transplacental viral transmission.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D. Antiviral prophylaxis only when cases of influenza occur in the residential facility (Choice A) is inappropriate, as immunization is more efficacious in the prevention of: Influenza virus immunization after delivery (Choice B) is inappropriate; the intramuscular inactivated vaccine is proven to be safe in pregnancy. Vaccines that only exist as live-attenuated versions, such as measles-mumps-rubella and varicella vaccines, should be administered after delivery. Influenza virus immunization after the influenza season begins (Choice C) would be an unnecessary delay in indicated care for this pregnant patient. Vaccination should be administered before the beginning of the season or as soon as possible if the season is already underway. Influenza virus immunization only when cases of influenza occur in the residential facility (Choice D) is not appropriate. Yearly influenza immunization is recommended for all patients regardless of exposure status and is especially important for pregnant patients due to the increased risk for serious complications.</p>", "17": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pelvic inflammatory disease caused by sexually transmitted infections such as chlamydia and gonorrhea may lead to infertility despite appropriate treatment. This occurs because of an immune response to the infection that causes scarring of the fallopian tubes, leading to fallopian occlusion and possible hydrosalpinx. The occlusion prevents the passage of ova and sperm, resulting in infertility.</p><p>Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) is characterized by inflammation of the upper genital tract and pelvis from ascending migration of a bacterial infection of the lower genital tract. Its clinical manifestations include fever, abdominopelvic pain, and mucopurulent vaginal discharge, as well as cervical motion and uterine tenderness on examination. Risk factors for its development include multiple sexual partners, failure to use a barrier method of contraception, and young age. Chlamydia trachomatis is a common cause of PID and can be treated with antibiotic therapy. Complications of PID include Fitz-Hugh—Curtis syndrome (or perihepatitis), tubo-ovarian abscess, and chronic issues with infertility caused by tubal occlusion. Tubal occlusion describes a lack of patent fallopian tubes, which is caused by an immune response to the infection and consequent scar formation, despite antibiotic treatment. The fallopian tubes can fill with fluid to form the bilateral hydrosalpinx seen in this patient. This occlusion prevents the passage of ova and sperm, resulting in infertility.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. h inhibit implantation and appropriate fetal growth. Genetic defects, such as gonadal dysgenesis 45,X (Turner syndrome), are also associated with infertility. However, congenital defects are an uncommon cause of hydrosalpinx and fallopian tube occlusion. Congenital defects (Choice A), especially those of the uterus, can lead to chronic infertility. Structural abnormalities may prevent implantation and appropriate fetal growth. Genetic defects, such as gonadal dysgenesis 45,X (Turner syndrome), may also cause infertility. Congenital defects are an uncommon cause of hydrosalpinx and fallopian tube occlusion. Ectopic endometrial tissue (Choice B) is the etiology of endometriosis. Endometriosis classically presents with dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and dyschezia. Endometriosis is caused by the implantation of ectopic endometrial tissue, and these implants can lead to tubal occlusion. However, this patient has had no symptoms of endometriosis to suggest that this is the cause of her infertility. Ovarian cancer (Choice D) classically presents indolently with abdominal distension, early satiety, and weight loss in a postmenopausal woman. Signs of advanced disease include bowel obstruction, ascites, pleural effusions, and venous thromboembolism. Imaging typically shows a unilateral, heterogeneous mass rather than bilateral hydrosalpinx and fallopian tube occlusion. Tubal spasm (Choice E) can cause temporary tubal occlusion as a result of smooth muscle contraction that prevents ova or sperm from passing through the fallopian tube. However, as it is only a temporary event, it would be unlikely to cause the 2 years of infertility seen in this patient.</p>", "18": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hyperthyroidism in pregnancy is most often caused by Graves disease or by stimulation of the TSH receptor by human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG). Presentation includes diaphoresis, weight loss, anxiety, palpitations, tremor, and tachycardia, in addition to exophthalmos in Graves disease. In normal pregnancy, increased thyroxine-binding globulin and circulating hCG can cause decreased TSH and increased total T3 and total Ty. Laboratory workup should therefore begin with measurement of free T4, which is increased in hyperthyroidism.</p><p>The presentation of thyrotoxicosis includes diaphoresis, weight loss, anxiety, palpitations, tremor, and tachycardia. Hyperthyroidism in pregnancy may occur as a result of Graves disease (likely in this patient given her exophthalmos) or can be mediated by human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), as the beta subunit of hCG has significant homology with TSH. Other causes of hyperthyroidism, such as toxic multinodular goiter, toxic thyroid adenoma, and thyroiditis, are less common in pregnancy. In nongravid patients, a decreased or undetectable TSH concentration is the most sensitive indicator of hyperthyroidism. However, TSH can be decreased in normal pregnancy due to an increase in serum thyroxine-binding globulin and stimulation of the TSH receptor by hCG. Evaluation of hyperthyroidism in pregnancy should therefore initially focus on measuring concentrations of free thyroxine (T4), which will be increased. Free triiodothyronine (T3) concentrations will be increased as well, but because Ty is the precursor to T3, measurement of Ty is preferred as its concentrations will be affected earlier in the disease course. Once the diagnosis is established, measurement of the thyroid receptor antibody is helpful in differentiating between Graves and hCG-mediated causes, as it is positive in most patients with Graves disease. Radioiodine uptake scans are contraindicated in pregnancy. Patients with symptomatic, moderate to severe hyperthyroidism benefit from treatment with oral beta-blockers such as metoprolol or propranolol, which should be weaned as soon as thyroid hormone synthesis is controlled with propylthiouracil (first trimester) or methimazole (second and third trimester). - the precursor to T3, its concentrations will be affected earlier in the disease course, and measurement of free T, is therefore preferred.</p><p>[…] globulin (Choice C) concentrations are increased in normal pregnancy due to increased estrogen concentrations. Evaluation of thyroxine-binding globulin is not useful in the investigation of thyrotoxicosis. Total thyroxine (Choice D), or total Ty, measures T4 bound to thyroxine-binding globulin in addition to free T4. Total T, concentrations are increased in normal pregnancy due to increased concentrations of thyroxine-binding globulin, but free T4 concentrations should not be affected in euthyroid patients. Measurement of free Ty is therefore a more appropriate test to investigate thyrotoxicosis. Total triiodothyronine (Choice E), or total T3, measures T3 bound to thyroxine-binding globulin in addition to free T3. Total Tz concentrations are increased in normal pregnancy due to increased concentrations of thyroxine-binding globulin, but free T3 concentrations should not be affected in euthyroid patients.</p>", "19": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Active labor occurs from the time the cervix is 4 to 6 cm dilated until it is completely dilated at 10 cm. It can be prolonged, when cervical dilation proceeds at a rate less than 1 to 2 cm per hour, or arrested, when cervical dilation does not change in 4 hours with adequate contractions or 6 hours with inadequate contractions. Prolongation is managed with oxytocin augmentation, whereas arrest necessitates cesarean delivery.</p><p>Labor consists of three defined stages. The first stage is divided into latent and active labor and encompasses the start of labor (contractions and cervical change) until the completion of cervical dilation and effacement. The second stage includes the time from complete cervical dilation to the baby’s birth. The third stage contains the delivery of the placenta. The first stage of labor is considered latent until the cervix is 4 to 6 cm dilated, after which the rate of cervical dilation increases, and the patient is considered to be in active labor. Prolongation or arrest of labor progression can occur throughout the labor process. The active stage of labor can be prolonged, which occurs when the cervix is dilating at less than 1 to 2 cm per hour, or arrested, in which case there is no cervical change for greater than 4 hours with adequate contractions or no cervical change for greater than 6 hours with inadequate contractions. Additionally, the second stage can be prolonged, which occurs after 2 to 3 hours, or arrested if descent of the fetus fails to occur or further pushing is unsuccessful. Prolongation and arrest can be caused by hypotonic contractions, cephalopelvic disproportion, and a nonocciput-anterior fetal presentation. Fetal birth weight, maternal BMI, and neuraxial anesthesia can also play a role. Prolongation of labor can be managed with oxytocin to augment uterine contractions and prostaglandins to assist in cervical ripening, whereas arrest of labor is managed with cesarean delivery.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E. contractions or cephalopelvic disproportion. However, this patient remains in the first stage of labor, making arrest of descent an incorrect answer. Arrest of descent (Choice A) can occur during the second stage of labor if the fetal head fails to engage or descend despite adequate pushing. It is associated with malpresentation or cephalopelvic disproportion. However, this patient remains in the first stage of labor. Cervical stenosis (Choice B) is induced by any inflammatory, scarring process and presents with dysmenorrhea or cyclical pelvic pain associated with light flow during menses as a result of narrowing of the cervical os. If it involves the uterine cavity, it may also cause amenorrhea, infertility, and recurrent pregnancy loss. Cervical stenosis is unlikely to be present in this patient as she has had a significant cervical dilation, which would not occur with cervical stenosis. Contracted pelvis (Choice C) refers to a pelvis that is smaller than expected in one or more dimensions. A contracted pelvis can be normal in a very petite person or abnormal as a result of inherited or acquired factors. A contracted pelvis most commonly inhibits the second stage of labor because of the inability of the fetal head to appropriately engage in the pelvis. This patient is still in the first stage of labor, making contracted pelvis incorrect. Prolonged latent phase (Choice E) of labor occurs when cervical dilation does not occur as rapidly as expected in the latent phase of labor, although the length of the latent phase is highly variable between patients. As this patient is already in active labor given her cervical dilation, prolonged latent phase is incorrect.</p>", "20": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Ductal papilloma is the most common cause of unilateral bloody nipple discharge. Ductal papillomata are benign tumors originating from the walls of lactiferous ducts that proliferate into the ductal lumen. A small portion of papillomata contain cellular atypia or ductal carcinoma in situ.</p><p>Ductal (or intraductal) papilloma is the most common cause of unilateral bloody nipple discharge, which may reflect malignant pathology if present in the setting of a palpable breast mass. Ductal papillomata are benign tumors originating from the walls of lactiferous ducts that proliferate into the ductal lumen. The papilloma is commonly too small to be palpated but may occasionally be felt beneath the areola. Ductal papillomata can be solitary or multiple. Initial diagnosis is typically made by ultrasonography and core needle biopsy. A small portion of ductal papillomata contain regions of atypical cells or ductal carcinoma in situ. Removal of the papilloma can be _ performed with surgical excision or microdochectomy (excision of a lactiferous duct), which facilitates detailed histopathologic analysis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, E, F, G, H, I, and J. Breast abscess (Choice A) presents with a focal collection of fluctuant, erythematous, and tender breast tissue. These symptoms are not present in this patient.: Breast cancer (Choice B) commonly presents in postmenopausal women with a palpable, painless breast lump, and microcalcifications or an infiltrative mass on screening mammography. It does not typically cause bloody discharge. The more likely cause in this patient is ductal papilloma, although this lesion may contain precancerous or cancerous cells. Cystosarcoma phyllodes (Choice C) is a benign tumor of the breast that presents with a large, rapidly growing tumor that commonly occurs in premenopausal women. This patient’s lesion is small and associated with bloody nipple discharge, making ductal papilloma more likely. Fat necrosis of the breast (Choice E) is often detected in the breast as a palpable nodularity in an area of previous trauma. Peripheral calcifications are often seen on imaging. This patient has no reported history of breast trauma. Fibroadenoma (Choice F) presents as a unilateral, nontender, mobile, rubbery mass in the breast, often in a younger woman. It is typically benign. It would not cause bloody discharge. Fibrocystic changes of the breast (Choice G) are a benign condition occurring in menstruating women that presents with multiple, bilateral, painful breast cysts. This patient has one small lesion. Galactocele (Choice H) is a retained collection of breast milk that presents as a breast lump typically following the cessation of lactation. It generally self-resolves. This patient has no recent history of breast-feeding. Inflammatory carcinoma (Choice I) is an aggressive form of breast cancer that presents with inflammatory changes of the breast (erythema, edema, tenderness). It does not present with a small breast mass or bloody discharge. Mastitis (Choice J) presents with erythema and induration of the breast in an area of tender, warm skin. It classically occurs in breast-feeding patients because of nipple trauma leading to inoculation from cutaneous or oral flora. This patient is not breast-feeding, making ductal papilloma the more likely cause.</p>", "21": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Polyhydramnios refers to an excess of amniotic fluid that is associated with fetal developmental defects that result in impaired swallowing, such as esophageal atresia, anencephaly, or duodenal atresia. Additional risk factors include maternal diabetes mellitus, maternofetal Rh incompatibility, and congenital infections. Polyhydramnios is defined sonographically as an amniotic fluid index greater than 25.</p><p>Polyhydramnios refers to an excess of amniotic fluid. Polyhydramnios is associated with fetal developmental defects that result in impaired swallowing, such as esophageal atresia, anencephaly, or duodenal atresia. Risk factors for polyhydramnios also include maternal diabetes mellitus, maternofetal Rh incompatibility, and congenital infections. As in this patient, the size of the gravid uterus may greatly exceed that which is expected for gestational age, which can lead to maternal symptoms of dyspnea through prevention of diaphragmatic excursion. Diagnosis is facilitated with ultrasonography, which will show an enlarged pool of amniotic fluid and may be used to assess the amniotic fluid index. Polyhydramnios is defined sonographically as an amniotic fluid index greater than 25. Treatment is supportive, although patients with severe symptoms may be treated with amniocentesis. Other complications include cord prolapse and placental abruption on rupture of membranes.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. Fetal growth restriction (Choice B) occurs when the fetus is unable to obtain enough nutrients for proper growth and is typically diagnosed on prenatal ultrasonography. At birth, it presents with decreased muscle mass with minimal subcutaneous fat, as well as a very low birth weight. This patient's ultrasound showed no evidence of fetal arowth restriction: malformations of the fetal genitourinary tract (eg, posterior urethral valves). Fetal renal agenesis (Choice C) would cause oligohydramnios, rather than polyhydramnios, because of a decreased amount of amniotic fluid. Other causes of oligohydramnios include prelabor rupture of membranes and malformations of the fetal genitourinary tract (eg, posterior urethral valves). Fetal Rh alloimmunization (Choice D) could occur if an Rh-negative fetus was exposed to maternal Rh-positive blood, which most often happens during delivery or during episodes of fetomaternal bleeding (eg, secondary to trauma). However, the immune system in a fetus is too immature to create a strong enough antibody response for this to carry any significance. Maternal trauma (Choice E) does not frequently lead to polyhydramnios. More commonly, it causes placental abruption or fetomaternal bleeding. The latter could lead to polyhydramnios if the mother was Rh-negative and had developed alloimmunization to Rh(D) antigens, which would consequently cause hydrops fetalis in the fetus. However, this patient is Rh(D)-positive, making maternal trauma an unlikely cause of polyhydramnios.</p>", "22": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Congenital adrenal hyperplasia results from a defect in adrenal steroid biosynthesis. Its most common form, 21-hydroxylase deficiency, may be classical or nonclassical and presents with increased levels of 17a-hydroxyprogesterone. Classical, caused by a complete absence of enzyme, presents with salt-wasting in infants or precocious puberty in children. Nonclassical is caused by a partial deficiency and presents later in life with oligomenorrhea and hyperandrogenism. Symptoms in adult patients may be treated with glucocorticoids, which decrease adrenal androgen synthesis by inhibiting adrenocorticotropic hormone release from the pituitary gland.</p><p>Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) is a defect in adrenal steroid biosynthesis, particularly of cortisol, with variable effects on mineralocorticoids and androgens. Due to decreased production of hormones, the adrenal gland is stimulated by the pituitary gland to increase hormone production, leading to adrenal hyperplasia, the namesake of this condition. The most common form of CAH is 21-hydroxylase deficiency, which has various presentations, and is associated with an increased level of 17a- hydroxyprogesterone, androstenedione, and testosterone. Classical CAH is caused by complete absence of the hormone and presents with salt wasting in a newborn or precocious puberty in a child, as well as virilization of infants with an XX karyotype (eg, clitoral hypertrophy, labial fusion) due to the increased levels of androgens. Other symptoms of classical CAH include hypotension, hyperkalemia, hypoglycemia, and diffuse skin hyperpigmentation. Nonclassical CAH is caused by a decreased hormone level and is typically asymptomatic in childhood. Rather, it presents in later childhood or early adulthood with signs of androgen excess, such as hirsutism, and oligomenorrhea, both of which are seen in this patient. Oral contraceptives and glucocorticoids can be used to manage the symptoms of CAH in adult patients. Glucocorticoids, such as the prednisolone therapy used in this patient, work by inhibiting adrenocorticotropic hormone release from the pituitary gland and consequently suppressing adrenal androgen synthesis.: prednisolone does not affect aromatase function.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Increased hepatic synthesis of sex hormone-binding globulin (Choice B) may improve this patient's symptoms by decreasing the amount of free androgen available. However, this is not a mechanism of action of prednisolone. Increased renal clearance of androgens (Choice C) is not the correct answer. While this may improve the patient's symptoms of hyperandrogenism, prednisolone does not improve renal clearance of androgens. Ovarian androgen synthesis (Choice D) would worsen this patient's symptoms, as she already has increased concentrations of androstenedione and testosterone. Additionally, prednisolone does not promote ovarian androgen synthesis.</p>", "23": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Neisseria gonorrhoeae may cause multiple clinical syndromes, such as urethritis, vaginitis, cervicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease, Fitz-Hugh—Curtis syndrome, disseminated gonorrhea, or septic arthritis. Disseminated gonorrhea is caused by invasion of gonorrhea into the bloodstream and presents classically with fever, migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis, and pustular lesions over the distal extremities. Diagnosis includes demonstration of N. gonorrhoeae in the blood, urogenital secretions, or synovial fluid. Treatment consists of ceftriaxone.</p><p>Neisseria gonorrhoeae is a gram-negative diplococcus that can present with a variety of clinical syndromes, such as urethritis, vaginitis, cervicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease, Fitz-Hugh—Curtis syndrome, disseminated gonorrhea, or septic arthritis. When causing vaginitis, the most common presentation, N. gonorrhoeae typically presents with mucopurulent discharge and pruritus, although patients may be asymptomatic. The infection can cause concomitant urethritis with dysuria or ascend to cause cervicitis or pelvic inflammatory disease. When the infection spreads to the bloodstream, it can cause disseminated gonorrhea, which classically presents with fever, migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis, and pustular lesions over the distal extremities. Often, urogenital symptoms are not evident at the time of disseminated gonorrhea diagnosis. Evaluation should include obtaining blood cultures, nucleic acid amplification testing of a urogenital specimen, and Gram stain and culture of synovial fluid. Treatment includes antibiotic therapy with ceftriaxone.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E. h Antistreptolysin O titer (Choice A) is used to confirm a recent infection with group A streptococcus. It is often used in the evaluation of acute rheumatic fever, which may present with migratory polyarthralgia, carditis, Sydenham chorea, subcutaneous nodules, erythema marginatum, and other systemic symptoms such as fever and malaise. While acute rheumatic fever can cause the arthritis found in this patient, it is not associated with pustular lesions or tenosynovitis. Serologic testing for syphilis (Choice B) can detect the presence of antibodies against Treponema pallidum, the causative agent of syphilis. Syphilis presents in multiple stages with varying symptoms, including primary with a painless chancre, secondary with fever, diffuse maculopapular rash, lymphadenopathy, and condylomata lata, and tertiary with tabes dorsalis, aortitis, and gummas. Syphilis does not typically cause arthralgias. Testing for Borrelia burgdorferi (Choice C) would be used in the diagnosis of Lyme disease. Lyme disease is typically transmitted by the /xodes scapularis tick, which is endemic to the Northeastern United States. It causes erythema chronicum migrans in its early stages, but may progress to cause carditis, facial nerve palsy, heart block, and arthritis. This patient has no history of a tick bite or erythema chronicum migrans, making Lyme disease unlikely. Testing for Staphylococcus aureus (Choice E) may be used in the diagnosis of toxic shock syndrome. This syndrome is classically associated with the continuous use of tampons or nasal packing and presents with fever, a diffuse erythematous macular rash, and distributive shock that can lead to multiorgan failure. This patient is hemodynamically stable and does not have the other stigmata of toxic shock syndrome.</p>", "24": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute appendicitis may occur in pregnant patients and presents with variably located right-sided pain based on the location of the appendix or periumbilical abdominal pain, along with nausea, anorexia, fever, leukocytosis, and tenderness to palpation. Fluid in the paracolic gutter on ultrasonography is suggestive of appendiceal rupture, which is managed with emergent surgical exploration.</p><p>Appendicitis typically presents with right lower quadrant abdominal pain that is often periumbilical at onset, with associated nausea, anorexia, fever, leukocytosis, and tenderness to palpation in the right lower quadrant on examination. In pregnancy, due to the location of the enlarging uterus, the appendix may be displaced superiorly as compared with that of a nongravid patient. This displacement limits the classic history and physical examination, which rely on a right lower quadrant position of the appendix. For example, initial periumbilical pain is attributed to vague, visceral innervation of the appendix being referred to the midline; once inflammation affects the parietal peritoneum, the pain becomes well-localized to the right lower quadrant, hence, the migratory character. McBurney point, a sensitive location for tenderness on palpation, is located at one-third the distance between the inguinal ligament and the umbilicus because this is the common position of the appendix. When displaced cephalad, the parietal peritoneal inflammation may no longer occur at the McBurney point, even migrating as cephalad as the right upper quadrant, mimicking diagnoses such as cholecystitis. Diagnosis is facilitated by CT scan (though less ideal in pregnancy because of the risk for teratogenicity to the fetus), MRI, or ultrasonography, which may show appendiceal enlargement, the presence of an appendicolith, appendiceal wall thickening, or periappendiceal fluid. Fluid in the paracolic gutter is suggestive of appendiceal rupture or perforation and is typically accompanied by rising fever and leukocytosis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Immediate surgical exploration and appendectomy is indicated to prevent […] Abruptio placentae (Choice A), the premature separation of the placenta from the uterus, presents with vaginal bleeding, severe uterine pain, and tetanic contractions, typically in the third trimester. It commonly results from lower abdominal trauma, smoking, or cocaine use. Due to the compromised fetal blood supply, it is an obstetric emergency. Braxton Hicks contractions (Choice C) are sporadic, irregular, and usually mild contractions that develop during the second or third trimester, without cervical changes. These contractions do not signify the onset of labor. This patient’s constant, focal abdominal pain, along with fever, nausea, and vomiting, are more concerning for an intra- abdominal pathology. Hepatitis (Choice D) presents with nausea, vomiting, jaundice, and right upper quadrant pain. Causes include viral infections, autoimmune diseases, medications, and alcohol. Laboratory features of acute hepatitis include increased transaminases, which are not seen in this case. Preterm labor (Choice E) occurs between 20 and 37 weeks of gestation with regular uterine contractions and accompanied cervical change. As preterm delivery is associated with a high degree of fetal morbidity and mortality, it is usually treated with tocolytic therapy, hydration, and bed rest, as well as corticosteroids to promote fetal lung maturation if gestational age is less than 34 weeks.</p>", "25": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Annual screening for Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae is recommended for all sexually active patients under age 25 years. Chlamydia typically presents with urethral or vaginal discharge, dysuria, urethritis, and dyspareunia. Gonorrhea commonly causes mucopurulent discharge and pruritus. However, both infections may also remain asymptomatic. Chlamydia is treated with azithromycin or doxycycline. Gonorrhea is treated with ceftriaxone.</p><p>Testing for Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae is recommended by the United States Preventive Services Task Force for all sexually active patients under age 25 years. C. trachomatis is a sexually transmitted infection that classically presents with urethritis, dysuria, dyspareunia, and urethral or vaginal discharge. N. gonorrhoeae is a sexually transmitted infection that presents most commonly with mucopurulent discharge and pruritus. However, both infections can also be asymptomatic, thus the recommendation for screening. Complications include cervicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease, tubo-ovarian abscess, and long-term issues with fertility. Diagnosis is made with a nucleic acid amplification test. Treatment consists of either azithromycin or doxycycline for chlamydia and ceftriaxone for gonorrhea.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E. Complete blood count (Choice A) is not necessary in this patient. A complete blood count is useful in the evaluation of infection, which may present with leukocytosis, and in anemia. This patient has no signs of infection, with her painful menstruation more likely secondary to primary dysmenorrhea. She also has no history of menorrhagia or other health issues that would predispose her to anemia. a pregnant patient can cause sensorineural deafness, neurodevelopmental delay, congenital cardiac defects, microcephaly. and extramedullary hematopoiesis in the fetus. As this patient consistently uses condoms, her risk for pregnancy is low. Determination of rubella status (Choice B) is not necessary at this visit but should be determined prior to pregnancy. Congenital rubella infection can cause sensorineural deafness, neurodevelopmental delay, congenital cardiac defects, microcephaly. and extramedullary hematopoiesis. Pregnancy test (Choice C) is also indicated for this patient but is not more appropriate at this visit than sexually transmitted infection testing based on this presentation. If the results of this patient's testing for C. trachomatis and N. gonorrhoeae are positive, pregnancy status should then be established to determine safe antibiotic choices. Urinalysis (Choice E) is used in the evaluation of suspected urinary tract infection or pyelonephritis. Uncomplicated cystitis presents with dysuria, frequency, urgency, and suprapubic discomfort, whereas pyelonephritis causes fever and flank pain in addition to these symptoms. This patient has no symptoms of a urinary tract infection, making a urinalysis unnecessary.</p>", "26": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Prenatal visits are an important aspect of ensuring a healthy pregnancy and delivery. They allow the healthcare provider to recognize risk factors for increased maternal and fetal morbidity and mortality and intervene where appropriate. Most commonly, for uncomplicated pregnancies, prenatal visits are recommended every 4 weeks until 28 weeks of gestation, every 2 weeks from 28 to 36 weeks of gestation, and then every week until delivery.</p><p>Prenatal visits allow a healthcare provider to optimize a patient's chances to have a successful pregnancy and healthy child. The purpose of the initial prenatal visits should be to identify risks to the patient or fetus and to intervene where possible to decrease these risks. Typical assessments during these visits include evaluating medical conditions (eg, obesity, diabetes, hypertension), social behaviors (eg, substance use), exposure to teratogens (eg, medications), genetic conditions or family history, and vaccination records. Laboratory evaluation commonly includes pregnancy confirmation, ABO and Rh(D) type and antibody screen, rubella and varicella serology to ensure immunity, sexually transmitted infection screening, and urine culture. Finally, interventions include folic acid supplementation, abstinence from substance use, medication changes as needed, optimization of medical issues, treatment of infections and asymptomatic bacteriuria, and early recognition of an increased tisk of fetal or maternal morbidity and mortality. Most commonly, for uncomplicated pregnancies, prenatal visits are recommended every 4 weeks until 28 weeks’ gestation, every 2 weeks from 28 to 36 weeks’ gestation, and then every week until delivery. During these later visits, screenings for fetal anomalies, gestational diabetes, and group B Streptococcus colonization, as well as the administration of Rh(D) immune globulin may occur. This patient has a healthy, early pregnancy and should return for another prenatal visit in 4 weeks.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. Measurement of fasting serum glucose concentration (Choice B) may be recommended during early prenatal visits for patients with known diabetes mellitus or with an increased risk for its development, neither of which this patient has. More commonly, a pregnant patient will undergo an oral glucose tolerance test between 24 and 28 weeks of gestation to evaluate for gestational diabetes. Gestational diabetes can lead to macrosomia, neonatal respiratory distress, and newborn hypoglycemia. Administration of Rh(D) immune globulin (Choice C) is recommended for Rh(D)-negative mothers at 28 weeks’ gestation as well as following the delivery of an Rh(D)- positive newborn, after any invasive procedure (eg, amniocentesis), and after any episodes of fetomaternal bleeding. Rh(D) immune globulin prevents the development of anti-D antibodies if a mother is exposed to Rh(D) antigens from the fetal circulation, as the development of these antibodies could lead to erythroblastosis fetalis in subsequent pregnancies if the fetus is Rh(D)-positive as the maternal antibodies can cross the placenta and attack the fetal erythrocytes. Administration of rubella vaccine (Choice D) is inappropriate in a pregnant patient, as it is a live attenuated virus. Live vaccines are contraindicated in pregnant patients as they can lead to infection of the fetus. Rubella infection of an unvaccinated pregnant patient can lead to fetal defects such as sensorineural deafness, neurodevelopmental delay, congenital cardiac defects, microcephaly. and extramedullary hematopoiesis. Therefore, this patient should be vaccinated after delivery if she is not immune. Administration of varicella vaccine (Choice E) is contraindicated in pregnant patients, as it is a live virus. Varicella infection in early pregnancy can cause congenital varicella syndrome, which is characterized by severe birth defects including microcephaly. intracranial calcifications, cerebral cortical atrophy, intellectual disability, seizures, ocular defects such as microphthalmia, cataracts, or chorioretinitis, limb hypoplasia, and cicatrizing skin lesions. Thus, this patient should be vaccinated after delivery if she is not immune.</p>", "27": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Prelabor rupture of membranes (PROM) refers to the rupture of the amniotic sac before the onset of labor. Patients often present with a gush of fluid from the vagina in the absence of uterine contractions. The diagnosis is confirmed with ultrasonography, as well as nitrazine testing and microscopic examination of the vaginal fluid. Treatment of PROM at term includes hospital admission for the induction of labor in order to prevent complications associated with prolonged rupture of membranes, such as chorioamnionitis and fetal infection.</p><p>Prelabor rupture of membranes (PROM) is the rupture of the amniotic sac before the onset of labor, defined as regular contractions with concomitant cervical change. Most commonly, patients report a gush of fluid or steady leakage of fluid from the vagina. Physical examination will typically show a closed cervix with pooling of fluid in the fornix; testing of the fluid will show that it is nitrazine positive and shows ferning on microscopic examination. Further assessment should include ultrasonography to evaluate for oligohydramnios and to determine fetal position, fetal heart rate monitoring, tocometry, and laboratory studies to rule out concomitant infection. Complications associated with PROM include intrauterine or fetal infection, such as chorioamnionitis, fetal malpresentation, and placental abruption. The risk for intrauterine infection is directly correlated to the length of time between membrane rupture and delivery. For this reason, most patients who present with PROM at term are admitted to the hospital for the induction of labor.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. h Streptococcus agalactiae (group B) colonization in order to prevent neonatal transmission. It is not typically given in a prophylactic manner for patients who present with PROM who are negative for S. agalactiae (group B). Admission to the hospital for intravenous ampicillin therapy (Choice B) is not appropriate. Ampicillin is used for patients with Streptococcus agalactiae (group B) colonization in order to prevent neonatal transmission. It is not typically indicated when group B streptococcal testing is negative. Admission to the hospital for intravenous clindamycin therapy (Choice C) can be used in the treatment of chorioamnionitis. Chorioamnionitis is a polymicrobial infection of the fetal membranes that commonly occurs in the setting of prolonged rupture of membranes as a result of ascension of the vaginal flora into the uterine cavity. Maternal fever, leukocytosis, and uterine tenderness are common. However, this patient has no signs of chorioamnionitis, so she does not warrant antibiotic therapy. Admission to the hospital for observation only (Choice D) or follow-up office examination in 1 day (Choice E) is inappropriate. Although many patients who present with PROM will progress into spontaneous labor, this occurs with varying amounts of delay based on the patient. As prolonged rupture of membranes is associated with significant risks for the mother and fetus, and this patient is gestationally at term, the most appropriate next step in management would be admission to the hospital for the induction of labor.</p>", "28": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Endometrial cancer can present with postmenopausal vaginal bleeding or abnormal uterine bleeding in premenopausal women. Risk factors for its development include prolonged, unopposed exposure to estrogen, such as early menarche, late menopause, chronic anovulatory cycles, exogenous estrogen therapy, nulliparity, and obesity. Diagnosis is made with endometrial biopsy, hysteroscopy, or dilatation and curettage.</p><p>Endometrial cancer most commonly presents with postmenopausal vaginal bleeding, although it can also present with abnormal uterine bleeding in premenopausal women. Abnormal uterine bleeding should be suspected when bleeding is irregular, unpredictable, or not associated with usual premenstrual signs and symptoms (eg, bloating, breast fullness, uterine cramps). Risk factors include prolonged, unopposed exposure to estrogen, such as early menarche, late menopause, chronic anovulatory cycles (eg, polycystic ovary syndrome), exogenous estrogen therapy, nulliparity, and obesity. The next most appropriate step in evaluation of patients with these presenting symptoms is an endometrial biopsy to evaluate for the presence of endometrial cancer. If a patient cannot tolerate the procedure or if the procedure sampling is nondiagnostic, a dilatation and curettage or a hysteroscopy can be performed. A hysteroscopy with sampling allows for a more complete evaluation of the endometrium and any focal lesions. This patient had early menarche and has had approximately 10 years of likely anovulatory cycles (ie, only menstruates twice yearly), which puts her at tisk for prolonged, unopposed estrogen exposure. This estrogen exposure leads to endometrial growth, hyperplasia, and carcinoma.</p><p>[…] B) is a risk factor for the development of endometrial cancer, as androgens are converted to estrogens in adipose tissue. This increases the amount of unopposed estrogen to which these patients are exposed. However, this patient’s BMI is within normal limits. Cigarette smoking (Choice C) has been associated with decreased risk for the development of endometrial cancer. By contrast, it is associated with an increased risk for developing cervical cancer and many other malignancies. Hypertension (Choice D) has not been shown to be directly associated with the development of endometrial cancer.</p>", "29": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Galactoceles are benign retention cysts of the breast caused by obstruction of the lactiferous ducts to the outflow of milk in patients who are lactating or shortly after the cessation of lactation. They most commonly present as a fluctuant, painless mass in the subareolar area. Most self-resolve; treatment is primarily supportive with massage and application of heat, although patients with refractory symptoms may benefit from therapeutic fine-needle aspiration.</p><p>A galactocele is a benign retention cyst of the mammary glands, caused by an obstruction to lactiferous duct outflow. Although the fluid in this case is described as clear, typically a galactocele is filled with milky fluid. They occur in lactating patients (or patients who recently discontinued lactating) and present with a firm, fluctuant mass that is painless or mildly tender and most commonly present in the subareolar area. Vital signs will be within normal limits. Diagnosis is typically clinical, but mammography or ultrasonography can be performed, which would show a cystic mass. Galactoceles generally resolve on their own, and treatment is primarily supportive with massage and application of heat. Patients with persistent, symptomatic galactoceles may benefit from aspiration or excision. Complications are uncommon but include superimposed infection and abscess formation, as well as rupture and subsequent inflammation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. Breast abscesses (Choice A) are infections of the breast tissue that occur primarily in breast-feeding women but also occur in nonlactating women. They present as y associated with fever and purulent nipple discharge. They can develop from galactoceles if bacteria are introduced through abrasions of the nipple, but this patient's galactocele has not progressed to an abscess. Mastitis is a common precursor. Fibroadenoma (Choice B) presents as a unilateral, nontender, mobile, rubbery mass in the breast, often in a younger woman. It is typically benign. Inflammatory carcinoma (Choice D) of the breast presents with unilateral breast edema, erythema, and pain, as well as a characteristic ‘peau d’orange’ (orange peel) appearance of the skin caused by invasion of dermal lymphatic channels, which blocks drainage of the breast. Inflammatory carcinoma is often misdiagnosed as mastitis, but it does not present with a discrete mass. Mastitis (Choice E) refers to a cellulitis of the breast tissue, usually caused by bacterial introduction at the nipple. It presents with unilateral erythematous, indurated, edematous, warm, tender breast tissue, and is frequently associated with fever. It is treated with continuation of breast-feeding and oral antibiotics. It does not present with a unilateral mass unless it has progressed to an abscess.</p>", "30": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Disseminated intravascular coagulation can be provoked by numerous causes in pregnancy, including placental abruption, postpartum hemorrhage, preeclampsia/eclampsia, HELLP syndrome, acute fatty liver of pregnancy, amniotic fluid embolism, and trauma. It is characterized by depletion of coagulation factors, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and bleeding. Treatment involves correcting coagulopathy, correcting thrombocytopenia if platelets are less than 50,000/mm°, and identifying and correcting the underlying cause.</p><p>Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is a systemic disorder characterized by the widespread activation of the clotting cascade resulting in microthrombi formation, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and bleeding complications (caused by consumption of platelets and clotting factors). Causes of DIC in pregnancy ~ laboratory abnormalities reflecting disseminated activation of the clotting cascade (increased aPTT, PT, and INR) and consumption of platelets (thrombocytopenia). i Additional laboratory abnormalities include increased D-dimer (and fibrin degradation products) and low fibrinogen concentrations, along with increased markers of hemolysis (detectable free hemoglobin, low haptoglobin, increased lactate dehydrogenase). Treatment includes correcting the underlying cause, and correction of resulting hematologic derangements such as coagulopathy, thrombocytopenia (if platelets are less than 50,000/mm’), and acute anemia. Coagulopathy can be treated with fresh frozen plasma (FFP) (which includes clotting factors, fibrinogen, antithrombin Ill, and plasma proteins), cryoprecipitate (concentrated fibrinogen), or prothrombin complex concentrates.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. Heparin (Choice B) results in immediate anticoagulation through binding and potentiating the action of antithrombin II], causing the inhibition of thrombin and factor Xa. It is commonly used for anticoagulation in the setting of deep venous thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, and acute coronary syndrome. Administration of heparin to this patient would worsen her coagulopathy. Oxytocin (Choice C) is used to induce uterine contraction in the setting of uterine atony and postpartum hemorrhage. While hemorrhage is a possible cause of DIC, the cause of this patient's DIC is not yet clear. Moreover, oxytocin can induce labor, which can lead to catastrophic hemorrhage in a coagulopathic patient. Correction of her coagulopathy with FFP should be prioritized in order to decrease further bleeding. Packed red blood cells (Choice D) are used to correct anemia caused by extensive blood loss. The typical transfusion threshold is a hemoglobin concentration of 7 mg/dL or lower, though a higher threshold can be used for patients who show signs of end-organ perfusion deficits (tachycardia, oliguria, increased creatinine, hypoxemia). While hemorrhage is a possible cause of this patient's DIC, her coagulopathy must first be corrected with FFP before the root cause can be further investigated and treated. Platelet (Choice E) transfusion is indicated when a patient's platelet concentration is less than 40,000/mm? without evidence of bleeding or 50,000/mm® when the patient is actively bleeding or requires a surgical procedure. This patient does not require a platelet transfusion at this time.</p>", "31": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Preeclampsia presents with hypertension, proteinuria, and end-organ dysfunction (eg, headache, blurred vision, right upper quadrant pain) after 20 weeks’ gestation caused by abnormalities of the placental spiral arteries. When untreated, it can cause eclampsia, characterized by generalized tonic-clonic seizures associated with significant fetal and maternal morbidity and mortality. The initial treatment for eclampsia is the administration of magnesium sulfate.</p><p>The cause of this patient’s generalized tonic-clonic seizure is most likely eclampsia, a complication of untreated preeclampsia. Preeclampsia is caused by an abnormality of the placental spiral arteries and poor trophoblastic invasion, leading to placental hypoperfusion and the subsequent production of vasoactive hormones that cause generalized endothelial dysfunction, hypercoagulability, and vasospasm. It usually presents in the second or third trimester after 20 weeks’ gestation with hypertension, proteinuria, and end-organ dysfunction (eg, headache, blurred vision, right upper quadrant pain), although it may also occur in the postpartum period. Preeclampsia may lead to uteroplacental insufficiency that puts the fetus at risk for fetal growth restriction. If untreated, preeclampsia can progress to eclampsia, characterized by maternal seizures, which can have profoundly adverse outcomes including maternal stroke, cardiac arrest, acute kidney injury, or maternal or fetal death. Preeclampsia is treated with antihypertensive agents, eclampsia prophylaxis with magnesium sulfate, and delivery of the fetus and placenta. Magnesium sulfate is the initial treatment for eclampsia as well, as it is thought to increase the seizure threshold and has been shown to improve maternal mortality. Therefore, the most appropriate next step in management of this patient is the administration of magnesium sulfate.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Incorrect Anewere: A RD and F: CT scan of the head (Choice B) may be warranted if this patient's seizures do not improve with magnesium. However, this patient has the classic symptoms of preeclampsia with hypertension, proteinuria, and end-organ dysfunction, making a seizure secondary to eclampsia more likely than one secondary to a central nervous system lesion. Lumbar puncture (Choice D) could be used to evaluate for central nervous system infections or other processes that may lead to generalized seizures, such as bacterial meningitis. However, this patient is afebrile, making infection less likely, and has signs and symptoms consistent with preeclampsia. Additionally, preeclampsia and eclampsia are often associated with thrombocytopenia, which may make a lumbar puncture unsafe. Immediate cesarean delivery (Choice E) may follow administration of magnesium sulfate. For patients at 34 weeks’ gestation, eclampsia is an indication for induction of labor if the mother can be stabilized with magnesium sulfate therapy and antihypertensive medications. For pregnancies that are earlier than this gestational age, cesarean delivery is often performed secondary to the low likelihood of successful vaginal delivery with induction.</p>", "32": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Primary dysmenorrhea is defined as pain that accompanies each menstrual cycle but is not associated with any underlying pathology. It is caused by uterine vasoconstriction in response to increased prostaglandin production by the endometrium. Treatment of primary dysmenorrhea involves administration of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and topical heat therapy, with oral contraceptives and progestin IUD as options for persistent symptoms.</p><p>Primary dysmenorrhea is defined as pain that accompanies each menstrual cycle but is not associated with any underlying pathology. In order to diagnose primary dysmenorrhea, causes of secondary dysmenorrhea must be ruled out. These may include endometriosis, leiomyomata uteri (uterine fibroids), adhesions, pelvic inflammatory disease, or adenomyosis. Many of these can be reasonably assessed on pelvic examination, which in this case did not show any abnormalities. Primary dysmenorrhea typically presents with pain in the lower abdomen and pelvis that may radiate to the lower back or thighs. It is often accompanied by nausea, diarrhea, headaches, and flushing. It can be attributed to uterine vasoconstriction in response to increased prostaglandin production by the endometrium. Thus, treatment targets this pathway with medications including nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and topical heat therapy. Oral contraceptives or a progestin IUD may also be used to regulate these symptoms if persistent.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E. Adenomyosis of the uterus (Choice A) is the presence of glandular endometrial tissue in the myometrium, and it generally presents with abnormal uterine bleeding and pelvic pain. It commonly occurs in a multigravid patient, often in the fourth or fifth decade of life. On examination, a large, globular uterus may be palpated. It would be unlikely in this vound patient.: as chlamydia or gonorrhea. This patient has cyclical pain with menstruation, making primary dysmenorrhea the more likely cause of her symptoms. Chronic pelvic inflammatory disease (Choice B) is an uncommon pelvic infection most often caused by Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoeae and may be associated with pelvic pain, dyspareunia, and low-grade fever. This patient has cyclical pain with menstruation, making primary dysmenorrhea more likely. Endometriosis of the pelvic peritoneum (Choice C) refers to the presence of endometrial tissue on the pelvic peritoneum. Endometriosis classically presents with symptoms of dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and dyschezia. Pelvic examination will often show tenderness of the uterine wall and surrounding structures with possible uterine immobility secondary to pelvic adhesions. This patient has no symptoms outside of her menstrual period, making endometriosis unlikely. Submucous leiomyomata uteri (Choice E), also known as uterine fibroids, are benign neoplasms of the uterine myometrium. They are a potential cause of abnormal uterine bleeding and pelvic pain. They may be palpated on examination if large. However, they would more likely present with persistent pain and would be unlikely to cause nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea.</p>", "33": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Episiotomy is now rarely used during delivery, most commonly reserved for the expedited delivery of newborns with a concerning fetal heart rate tracing or to facilitate operative vaginal delivery with forceps or vacuum. Complications include infection, bleeding, extension of the incision, a narrowed introitus with consequent dyspareunia, and severe tears with subsequent pregnancies. Infections present with fever, pain, induration, and erythema over the incision. Treatment includes broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy, as well as possible incision and drainage.</p><p>An episiotomy is a now rarely used incision performed by an obstetrician during delivery that is used to aid in fetal delivery. It may be midline, extending from the posterior introitus toward the anus, or mediolateral, extending from the posterior introitus to the ischial tuberosities. More recently, it has been reserved for the expedited delivery of newborns with a concerning fetal heart rate tracing or to facilitate operative vaginal delivery with forceps or vacuum. Complications of episiotomy include infection, seen in this patient, as well as bleeding, extension of the incision, a narrowed introitus after repair with consequent dyspareunia, and severe tears with subsequent pregnancies. Episiotomy infections present similarly to other wound infections with pain, induration, and erythema. Fever and purulent drainage from the site may also be present. Treatment includes broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy, as well as possible incision and drainage of the wound.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. Intersphincteric anal abscess (Choice B), ischiorectal fossa abscess (Choice C), and perirectal abscess (Choice D) are all similar processes that typically originate from obstruction of an anal gland and/or an anal fistula. This obstruction and consequent inflammation and infection is broadly defined as a perirectal abscess, which is further delineated based on location (perianal, intersphincteric, or ischiorectal). Perirectal abscesses present with perirectal pain, fever, and possibly purulent drainage. Physical examination may show skin changes such as induration and erythema (perianal abscess) or areas of fluctuance. This patient has no area of fluctuance on digital rectal examination, making any of these abscesses unlikely. Rectovaginal fistula (Choice E) is a fistula formed between the rectum and vagina, which most commonly presents with foul-smelling vaginal discharge, dyspareunia, and the passage of gas or stool from the vagina. It can occur as a complication of childbirth or from other sources of pelvic trauma (eg, surgery, radiation therapy, chronic inflammation). It would not present with incisional pain, induration, or erythema.</p>", "34": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Preterm labor refers to labor with an onset before 37 weeks’ gestation. Risk factors include prior preterm labor, maternal diabetes, extremes of age, placenta previa or abruption, cervical insufficiency, multiple gestations, and maternal tobacco use. Progesterone supplementation (eg, 17a-hydroxyprogesterone therapy) has been used for patients with a history of preterm delivery to prevent its recurrence. For patients who present in preterm labor, treatment includes bed rest, hydration, and tocolysis, as well as corticosteroids and magnesium depending on the gestational age.</p><p>Normal labor and delivery typically occur after 37 weeks’ gestation with the onset of contractions associated with cervical dilation and the rupture of membranes. Labor that begins prior to 37 weeks’ gestation is referred to as preterm labor. Risk factors include prior preterm deliveries, maternal diabetes, extremes of age, placenta previa or abruption, cervical insufficiency, multiple gestations, and maternal tobacco use. The risk for complications in the fetus increases the earlier the preterm labor begins, with these complications including respiratory distress syndrome, hypothermia, hypoglycemia, intraventricular hemorrhage, and necrotizing enterocolitis. Treatment of preterm labor includes tocolytic therapy, hydration, and bed rest. Corticosteroids are also given to promote fetal lung maturation if the gestational age is less than 34 weeks, and magnesium is added for neuroprotection in patients with a gestational age less than 32 weeks. For patients with a history of or at high risk for preterm labor and delivery, such as this patient, progesterone supplementation (eg, 17a-hydroxyprogesterone therapy) has been used with some success to prevent preterm labor. Progesterone supplementation is typically started at 16 to 20 weeks’ gestation and continued until term. It is thought to inhibit uterine contractility in support of the developing fetus.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. Bed rest (Choice A) is an inappropriate recommendation. […] Cervical cerclage (Choice B) refers to the placement of a suture into the cervical os to narrow the canal and maintain a closed cervix. A cervical cerclage is used in the treatment of cervical insufficiency, or the inability of the cervix to contain the pregnancy without the presence of contractions, labor, or both. It is not useful in the prevention of preterm labor. Nifedipine therapy (Choice D) and terbutaline therapy (Choice E) are forms of tocolytic therapy used in the treatment of preterm labor. These agents act to relax the uterine smooth muscle and decrease the frequency and strength of uterine contractions. While tocolysis is an appropriate intervention for the treatment of a patient who presents in active preterm labor, it plays no role in its prevention.</p>", "35": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Rubella infection in a pregnant patient increases the risk for congenital rubella infection, which characteristically presents with cataracts, sensorineural deafness, and congenital heart disease (eg, patent ductus arteriosus) in the newborn, as well as microcephaly. neurodevelopmental delay, and extramedullary hematopoiesis.</p><p>Rubella infection is spread by respiratory droplets and can be asymptomatic or present with an erythematous rash, arthralgias, postauricular lymphadenopathy, and fever. In otherwise healthy patients, the infection is usually self-limited with no long-term sequelae. In pregnant patients, however, especially when the infection occurs in the first trimester, it can cause an infection of the fetus that manifests with cataracts, sensorineural deafness, and congenital cardiac defects (eg, patent ductus arteriosus), as well as microcephaly. neurodevelopmental delay, and extramedullary hematopoiesis, which manifests as a blue-purple maculopapular rash. Infection with the virus is prevented by administration of the measles-mumps-rubella (MMR) vaccination prior to conception, as the vaccination is a live-attenuated vaccine, making it contraindicated during pregnancy. Ideally, confirmation of rubella immunity with measurement of serum titers should be achieved prior to pregnancy for patients of reproductive age.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E. Administration of antibiotics during pregnancy (Choice A) would not have been likely to prevent this finding; most infectious causes of microcephaly are viral (eg, rubella, cytomegalovirus, Zika, herpes simplex, and HIV) or parasitic (eg, toxoplasmosis) and thus would not respond to antibiotics. Pregnant patients who do present with symptoms concerning for any of the above infections should be evaluated and promptly treated. Prepregnancy vaccination is the most effective method of prevention of rubella infection.: polyhydramnios, intrauterine growth restriction, low birthweight, and preterm birth, as well as neuropsychiatric abnormalities in the fetus including autism and learning disabilities. Iron deficiency anemia has not been linked to microcephaly. Folic acid supplementation during pregnancy (Choice B) is useful for the prevention of neural tube defects but would not prevent congenital rubella syndrome or microcephaly from rubella infection. Iron supplementation during pregnancy (Choice C) is useful for the prevention of iron deficiency anemia and is not associated with prevention of microcephaly. Progesterone supplementation prior to conception (Choice E) would not have prevented this developmental abnormality. Progesterone supplementation in early pregnancy is appropriate for patients with recurrent pregnancy loss to support the pregnancy until the placenta begins secreting progesterone. Supplementation before conception would prevent ovulation and pregnancy, as progestin can be used on its own as a hormonal contraceptive.</p>", "36": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Cervical cancer screening is recommended to be completed with cervical cytology via Pap smear every 3 to 5 years depending on the patient's age. If cytology results show evidence of a high-grade lesion, the next most appropriate step in management is colposcopy and biopsy. Patients who have invasive lesions as evidenced by histology should undergo hysterectomy.</p><p>Cervical cancer classically presents with vaginal bleeding (with or without sexual intercourse) and an exophytic or friable cervical lesion, although it may also be asymptomatic. Risk factors for cervical cancer include infections of human papillomavirus (HPV) high-risk strains 16, 18, 31, or 33, early age at onset of sexual activity, tobacco use, oral contraceptive use, immunosuppression, and infection with HIV or other sexually transmitted infections. In many cases, the carcinoma begins in the transition zone of the cervix, which is the target region of a Pap smear, a cytologic screening test of cervical neoplasia. Pap smears are recommended every 3 years for women age 21 to 29 years and every 5 years in conjunction with HPV cotesting (every 3 years if cotesting is not performed) in women age 30 to 65 years. For an invasive lesion, treatment includes hysterectomy as well as possible chemoradiation depending on final staging. This patient has microinvasive squamous cell carcinoma, making hysterectomy the most appropriate next step in management in order to ensure complete removal of the cancer.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, E, F, and H. Colposcopic laser ablation (Choice A) and cryotherapy (Choice E), different ablation techniques, are treatment options for high-grade histology as confirmed by colposcopy with biopsy. They are appropriate for small lesions that do not extend into the cervical canal, but they are less commonly used for management than excisional procedures. Ablative techniques would not fully treat this patient's microinvasive tumor. Endometrial ablation (Choice B) is a potential treatment for menorrhagia in a premenopausal woman. As menorrhagia may also be caused by endometrial hyperplasia or carcinoma, all women undergoing endometrial ablation should have an endometrial biopsy first to exclude the presence of these conditions. This patient’s vaginal bleeding is secondary to cervical cancer, for which endometrial ablation would be unnecessary. Hysteroscopic excision (Choice C) refers to the use of a small camera to visualize the cervix and uterine cavity facilitating removal of an abnormal lesion. It may be used in the treatment of polyps, fibroids, or intrauterine septations. It would not effectively treat this patient’s cervical cancer. Laparoscopic excision (Choice D) would not be the most appropriate next step in management of this case. Laparoscopic excision is an appropriate therapy for endometriosis. While a hysterectomy could be accomplished laparoscopically and would be an appropriate treatment for this patient, a simple laparoscopic excision would be insufficient. Uterine artery embolization (Choice F) is most used in the treatment of heavy uterine bleeding, especially in the setting of fibroids or postpartum hemorrhage. It is typically reserved for patients who have completed childbearing as it may affect future fertility. It is not an appropriate treatment for carcinoma in situ of the cervix. Myomectomy (Choice H) refers to the surgical removal of a uterine fibroid, which can present with abnormal bleeding that may be irregular or heavy. This patient's cervical lesion is cervical cancer, not a fibroid.</p>", "37": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Fetal goiters can be caused by inborn errors of thyroid hormone metabolism, developmental abnormalities of the thyroid, iodine excess or deficiency, transplacental passage of maternal thyroid-stimulating antibodies, or transplacental passage of antithyroid drugs such as propylthiouracil. Large goiters can compress the esophagus, leading to polyhydramnios, or the airway, leading to respiratory distress at birth.</p><p>A mass in the fetal neck in the setting of maternal thyroid pathology is most likely a fetal goiter. Fetal goiters can be caused by inborn errors of thyroid hormone metabolism, developmental abnormalities of the thyroid (eg, large thyroglossal duct cysts), iodine excess or deficiency, transplacental passage of maternal thyroid-stimulating antibodies (eg, maternal Graves disease), or transplacental passage of antithyroid drugs such as propylthiouracil (PTU). Ultrasonography of this patient also shows polyhydramnios, an excess amount of amniotic fluid, defined as an amniotic fluid index greater than or equal to 24 cm. Polyhydramnios can be caused by fetal developmental defects that impair swallowing of amniotic fluid such as esophageal atresia, duodenal atresia, anencephaly, or, as in this patient, external compression of the esophagus (eg, by the goiter). Transplacental passage of antibodies can cause transient hyperthyroidism at birth until the antibodies are cleared; this can occur even in patients who are well controlled with antithyroid medication or thyroid ablation, as this does not affect levels of circulating antibodies. However, prenatal exposure to PTU can cause transient hypothyroidism at birth until the drug is cleared. Thus, it is difficult to predict whether this patient will be born hyperthyroid, hypothyroid, or euthyroid. Regardless, this hormonal derangement is usually self-limited and resolves without treatment. Severe disease can be managed with methimazole or levothyroxine, respectively. Given the size of the neck mass and its compressive force on neck structures, the infant may need supportive care to protect their airway and assist with feeding until the goiter has decreased in size. case with maternal thyroid pathology and use of antithyroid medications, fetal goiter is more likely.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> Fetal growth restriction (Choice C) is defined as fetal weight less than the 10th percentile for gestational age. Fetal growth restriction can be caused by conditions that lead to neck masses and polyhydramnios, but it does not cause these conditions. Moreover, this patient does not meet criteria for fetal growth restriction. Gestational diabetes (Choice D) can cause polyhydramnios, along with macrosomia and hyaline membrane disease as a result of increased fetal exposure to insulin. However, it is not associated with fetal neck masses, and a goiter is more likely in the setting of maternal thyroid pathology. Tracheoesophageal fistula (Choice E) often occurs concomitantly with esophageal atresia and is thus associated with polyhydramnios, as the esophageal atresia impairs the fetus’ ability to swallow amniotic fluid. Tracheoesophageal fistulas are usually not visible on prenatal ultrasonography and do not present as visible neck masses.</p>", "38": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Placenta previa refers to the abnormal positioning of placental tissue such that the placenta partially or completely overlies the internal cervical os. Risk factors for placenta previa include causes of uterine scarring, such as multiparity, previous cesarean delivery, and prior dilatation and curettage. The treatment of an initial bleed involves the administration of intravenous fluids and observation, whereas immediate cesarean delivery is recommended for patients in active labor and for those with uncontrollable bleeding.</p><p>Placenta previa refers to the abnormal positioning of placental tissue such that the placenta partially or completely overlies the internal cervical os. It most commonly presents with painless third trimester vaginal bleeding. Frequently, it is detected on prenatal ultrasonography, but it may not always be evident. Thus, patients who present with vaginal bleeding after 20 weeks’ gestation should not undergo a digital cervical examination until ultrasonography has been performed to determine the exact positioning of the placenta because severe hemorrhage can occur if a placenta previa is present. Risk factors for placenta previa include causes of uterine scarring, such as multiparity, previous cesarean delivery, and prior dilatation and curettage. While it may self-resolve as pregnancy progresses, its presence in the late third trimester requires careful management. Treatment of an initial bleed without significant hemodynamic instability, such as in this patient, necessitates intravenous administration of fluids and observation. Patients who are able to be stabilized may be expectantly managed with a plan for a cesarean delivery around 37 weeks’ gestation, to allow time for sufficient fetal development. A cesarean delivery is indicated to prevent significant hemorrhage, maternal/fetal complications, and potential fetal demise. Immediate cesarean delivery is advised for patients who present in labor or those with hemodynamically significant bleeding. Incorrect Anewere: A CD and FE: associated complications.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> More importantly, a vaginal delivery is contraindicated in the setting a placenta previa as it could result in severe hemorrhage Intravenous administration of magnesium sulfate (Choice C) is the treatment of choice for preeclampsia, which presents with hypertension, proteinuria, and end-organ dysfunction (eg, headache, blurred vision, right upper quadrant pain) after 20 weeks’ gestation because of abnormalities of the placental spiral arteries. When untreated, it can cause eclampsia, characterized by generalized tonic-clonic seizures. Preeclampsia does not typically cause vaginal bleeding. Amnioinfusion (Choice D) refers to the process of instilling fluid into the uterus during labor. It is often used to improve the presence of variable decelerations on fetal heart monitoring secondary to cord compression associated with low amniotic fluid during contractions. It is not used in the treatment of placenta previa. Immediate cesarean delivery (Choice E) is not necessary if the patient remains hemodynamically stable and her bleeding is able to be controlled. Placenta previa does necessitate immediate cesarean delivery if a patient with a known placenta previa presents in active labor or with uncontrollable bleeding associated with hemodynamic instability.</p>", "39": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Polycystic ovary syndrome presents with chronic anovulation, signs of androgen excess, obesity, insulin resistance, and polycystic ovaries on ultrasonography. Laboratory studies will show increased concentrations of luteinizing hormone and testosterone. Treatment includes weight loss and oral contraceptives for menstrual regulation, along with antihyperglycemic medications or insulin for the management of concomitant diabetes mellitus, if diagnosed.</p><p>Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) presents with irregular menses, infertility, and hirsutism, often in patients with an increased body weight. It requires two of the following for diagnosis: oligo/anovulation, polycystic ovaries on ultrasonography, and clinical or biochemical signs of hyperandrogenism. It is associated with signs of insulin resistance, such as acanthosis nigricans and increased blood glucose concentrations. PCOS is associated with an abnormal hypothalamic hormonal feedback response, which leads to increased luteinizing hormone (LH) in comparison to follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), as well as markedly increased androgen production from the theca lutein cells. This results in an increased serum concentration of testosterone. Treatment includes weight loss, oral contraceptives for menstrual regulation, and spironolactone or ketoconazole for hirsutism. Clomiphene stimulates ovulation and can also be used for patients desiring pregnancy. If the patient is diagnosed with diabetes mellitus, oral medications (eg, metformin) or insulin may also be necessary. A potential complication of long-standing PCOS is endometrial cancer due to the chronic anovulation resulting in prolonged exposure of the endometrium to unopposed estrogen.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E. they are in when testing occurs. However, because they spend more time in the follicular phase due to anovulation, when estradiol concentrations are the highest, they have a higher long-term exposure to estradiol than a patient without PCOS. Estradiol (Choice A) concentrations vary based on the phase of the menstrual cycle. Patients with polycystic ovary syndrome can have variable concentrations when testing occurs. However, because they spend more time in the follicular phase due to anovulation, they have a higher long-term exposure to estradiol than patients without PCOS. Follicle-stimulating hormone (Choice B) is increased in menopause and premature ovarian failure but is frequently normal in PCOS. LH, in contrast, is increased in PCOS as a result of decreased inhibition from the hypothalamic feedback loop. This increased LH is in part responsible for the excessive androgen production seen in PCOS. Prolactin (Choice C) abnormalities can occur in the setting of a prolactinoma, or prolactin-secreting tumor of the pituitary gland. Hyperprolactinemia inhibits gonadotropin- releasing hormone, as well as FSH and LH. It can present with oligomenorrhea but is often associated with galactorrhea, headaches, or visual changes, none of which are present in this patient. Thyroid-stimulating hormone (Choice E) could be used to evaluate the patient for hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism. Hypothyroidism presents with fatigue, weight gain, constipation, cold intolerance, oligomenorrhea, bradycardia, dry and edematous skin, and delayed relaxation of deep tendon reflexes. In contrast, hyperthyroidism presents with heat intolerance, weight loss, palpitations, diaphoresis, and diarrhea. While both may cause oligomenorrhea or amenorrhea, the patient has no other signs or symptoms to suggest one of these diseases.</p>", "40": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Rh(D)-negative mothers are at a high risk for Rh(D) alloimmunization when exposed to Rh(D) antigens from the fetal circulation. This can occur during episodes of fetomaternal bleeding, such as that secondary to a threatened abortion (eg, vaginal bleeding with a closed os prior to 20 weeks’ gestation), during delivery, or during any invasive procedure (eg, amniocentesis). Alloimmunization can be prevented by the administration of Rh(D) immune globulin at 28 weeks’ gestation, along with following the delivery of an Rh(D)-positive newborn, any invasive procedure, and any episodes of fetomaternal bleeding.</p><p>Rh(D)-negative mothers are at a high risk for developing anti-D antibodies if exposed to blood from an Rh(D)-positive fetus, which most commonly occurs during delivery. However, it can also occur during episodes of fetomaternal bleeding during pregnancy, such as that seen in this patient, likely secondary to a threatened abortion (eg, vaginal bleeding with a closed os prior to 20 weeks’ gestation). If Rh(D) antigens from the fetal circulation enter the maternal circulation, it is possible that the mother’s immune system will form antibodies against the Rh(D) antigen. This can result in severe hemolytic anemia of the newborn (eg, erythroblastosis fetalis) if the fetus is Rh(D)- positive, as maternal antibodies cause hemolysis of fetal erythrocytes when they cross the placenta. Rh(D) immune globulin acts by binding to Rh(D)-positive cells that enter the maternal circulation, preventing the immune system from developing antibodies to the Rh(D) antigen. Administration of Rhg(D) immune globulin is typically performed for Rh(D)-negative mothers at 28 weeks’ gestation, along with following the delivery of an Rh(D)-positive newborn, any invasive procedure (eg, amniocentesis), and any episodes of fetomaternal bleeding. Therefore, the most appropriate next step in management is to determine this patient's blood type and Rh factor, so she may be given Rh(D) immune globulin if necessary.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. Measurement of serum β-hCG concentration (Choice B) is most commonly used to confirm pregnancy, although serial measurement is not necessary in this patient because ultrasonography already shows a gestational sac with a live fetus. Intramuscular medroxyprogesterone therapy (Choice C) and oral progesterone therapy (Choice D) can be used as hormonal contraceptives. Progesterone acts to maintain the secretory endometrium and prepare it for embryo implantation, as well as decreases uterine contractility in support of the developing embryo. As such, progesterone therapy is occasionally used to prevent recurrent early pregnancy loss or preterm labor in those at increased risk. Progesterone would not be appropriate in this primigravid patient. Dilatation and curettage (Choice E) is used to treat heavy or abnormal uterine bleeding that does not respond to less invasive interventions, as well as for removing fetal tissue or retained products of conception. This would be inappropriate at this time, assuming that this is a desired pregnancy. Additionally, determination of this patient's blood type and Rh factor is a more time-sensitive intervention.</p>", "41": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Anovulation presents with irregular, often heavy menstrual periods caused by continued development of the endometrium between menstrual cycles. Anovulation is associated with a persistently high concentration of estrogen, as the endometrium remains in the follicular phase. This unopposed estrogen exposure puts patients with chronic anovulation at increased risk for endometrial hyperplasia and cancer.</p><p>Anovulation presents frequently with a missed menstrual period, followed by breakthrough bleeding at an irregular time in the cycle that is often heavy and prolonged. Anovulation can be caused by pregnancy, polycystic ovary syndrome, abnormalities or immaturity of the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis, menopause or premature ovarian failure, inadequate nutrition, chromosomal abnormalities, and thyroid disorders. As ovulation does not occur, the endometrium remains in the follicular phase with high concentrations of estradiol. This chronic, unopposed exposure to estrogen increases the risk for endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma. This patient’s morbid obesity increases her risk further, as androgens are converted to estrogens in the peripheral adipose tissue. Endometrial cancer commonly presents with vaginal bleeding in a postmenopausal patient. Risk factors in addition to those seen in this patient include early menarche, late menopause, and exogenous estrogen therapy. Diagnosis is made with endometrial biopsy, and treatment consists of hysterectomy plus chemotherapy or radiation depending on the stage.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. Breast (Choice A) cancer has similar risk factors as those of endometrial cancer. Both endogenous and exogenous sources of prolonged estrogen exposure, such as early menarche, late menopause, obesity, and nulliparity, increase the risk for breast cancer. However, chronic anovulation has a stronger association with endometrial cancer than with breast cancer. Colon (Choice B) cancer is most often diagnosed on routine screening in adults aged 50 to 75 years, although it may present with symptoms of changes in stool frequency or caliber, gastrointestinal bleeding, weight loss, and iron deficiency anemia. Chronic anovulation is not necessarily associated with the development of colon cancer. Ovarian (Choice D) cancer classically presents indolently in an older, postmenopausal female with abdominal distension and weight loss. Risk factors for the development of ovarian cancer include genetic variants such as BRCA1 and BRCA2, old age, nulliparity, early menarche, or late menopause. This patient's chronic anovulation and obesity put her at greater risk for developing endometrial cancer than of developing ovarian cancer. Pancreatic (Choice E) cancer commonly presents at a late stage with abdominal pain, weight loss, nausea, anorexia, and jaundice. Risk factors for its development include cigarette smoking, diabetes mellitus, obesity, and a family history.</p>", "42": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Breast cancer may present with a palpable breast lump or be diagnosed on screening mammography. If diagnostic or screening imaging shows findings concerning for breast cancer, such as a spiculated border or microcalcifications, core-needle biopsy should be performed to obtain a tissue diagnosis. If core- needle biopsy is nondiagnostic or the lesion is inaccessible percutaneously, surgical excisional biopsy should be performed.</p><p>Breast cancer is the most common nondermatologic malignancy in women, and commonly presents with a breast mass, skin or nipple retraction, or axillary lymphadenopathy. However, it may also be asymptomatic and diagnosed on screening mammography, as it was in this patient. Risk factors for breast cancer development include exogenous estrogen administration, a family history of premenopausal breast cancer, genetic mutations (eg, BRCA7 and BRCA2), early menarche, late menopause, nulliparity, obesity, and African American ethnicity. A family history of multiple first-degree relatives, particularly when the relatives are premenopausal at diagnosis, greatly increases the risk. Evaluation of a palpable breast mass first involves imaging with either mammography or ultrasonography to evaluate whether the palpable mass has features concerning for breast cancer, such as spiculated margins or microcalcifications. If these features are present on diagnostic or screening imaging, ultrasound-guided core-needle biopsy should be performed to obtain a tissue diagnosis. Surgical biopsy may be performed if core-needle biopsy is nondiagnostic or if the lesion would be difficult to access percutaneously. Treatment of breast cancer depends on the clinical stage and may involve surgical excision, chemotherapy, or radiation therapy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D. MRI of the left breast (Choice A) is unlikely to be of additional benefit for this patient. She has already had an imaging study that showed a mass concerning for breast: mass, which is typically reserved for cases in which the core-needle biopsy is nondiagnostic or for cases in which the lesion is difficult to access percutaneously. This patient’s breast lesion is in an accessible area, making core-needle biopsy the more appropriate choice as it is a less invasive option to diagnose the lesion. Needle-guided excisional biopsy (Choice B) is not the most appropriate next step in diagnosis of this mass. Excisional biopsy is typically reserved for cases in which core-needle biopsy is nondiagnostic or for masses in an inaccessible area, making core-needle biopsy the more appropriate choice. Repeat clinical examination in 3 months (Choice C) is inappropriate. This patient has a mass on mammography that is concerning for malignancy. Failing to properly categorize this lesion as benign or malignant with core-needle biopsy would greatly increase this patient's risk of morbidity and mortality associated with a delayed diagnosis of breast cancer. Sentinel lymph node biopsy (Choice D) is not appropriate as a diagnostic modality. Sentinel lymph node biopsy includes injecting a dye near the breast lesion and then using the dye to identify the lymph nodes that drain the area. These lymph nodes are then biopsied to determine whether breast cancer has metastasized. This patient should undergo diagnostic biopsy of the lesion before a sentinel lymph node biopsy for staging.</p>", "43": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Late decelerations are defined as gradual decreases in the fetal heart rate that begin after the peak of uterine contraction. These decelerations are caused by uteroplacental insufficiency that results in consequent fetal hypoxemia and acidemia. Risk factors include hypertension, diabetes, cocaine or tobacco use, coagulopathies, prolonged uterine contraction, uterine infection, late-term pregnancy, and maternal hypotension or hypoxia.</p><p>A healthy fetal heart rate is between 110 and 160 beats per minute (bpm) with 6 to 25 bpm of variability and spontaneous accelerations. Intermittent early and variable decelerations can also occur during the normal process of labor as a result of head compression and umbilical cord compression, respectively. In comparison, uteroplacental insufficiency and fetal hypoxemia can present with late decelerations on the fetal heart rate tracing, which are defined as gradual decreases in the fetal heart rate that begin after the peak of uterine contraction and nadir more than 30 seconds after the initiation of heart rate decline. Risk factors for the development of uteroplacental insufficiency are hypertension, diabetes, anemia, cocaine or tobacco use, coagulopathies, late-term pregnancy, and uterine infection. Fetal hypoxemia can also be caused by severe uterine contractions and maternal hypotension or hypoxia. Treatment consists of supplemental oxygenation, movement of the patient onto her left side or onto all fours, and intravenous fluids. If late decelerations persist on fetal heart rate tracings, then an urgent cesarean delivery is indicated, as continued uteroplacental insufficiency and fetal hypoxemia will result in deleterious effects on the fetus. This patient progressed through labor having consistent late decelerations, indicating that the fetus was probably experiencing recurrent fetal hypoxemia and consequent fetal metabolic acidemia, which are likely responsible for this newborn’s presentation on delivery.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.; Fetal respiratory acidosis (Choice B) and fetal respiratory alkalosis (Choice C) are unlikely causes of the findings. The fetus exhibits breathing movements in the amniotic sac, but no gas exchange occurs via these movements, so they do not result in a fetal respiratory acid-base disorder. Maternal lactic acidosis (Choice D) can occur in the setting of hypotension or hypoxemia, most frequently because of underlying infection in the intrapartum patient. This patient is healthy, has normal vital signs, and has no indication of an infection. Maternal lactic acidosis is unlikely to cause late decelerations on fetal heart rate monitoring unless severe and associated with other vital sign changes. Maternal respiratory alkalosis (Choice E) is unlikely in this patient. She is breathing 20 breaths per minute, which is at the upper limit of normal. Regardless, maternal changes in respiratory acid-base status are unlikely to cause late decelerations that lead to fetal hypoxemia and acidemia.</p>", "44": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Prepregnancy visits allow a patient to optimize their chances of having a successful pregnancy with a healthy child. Important aspects of these visits include ascertaining immunity to varicella and rubella, encouraging abstinence from substance use, changing teratogenic medications, and prescribing folic acid supplementation to prevent neural tube defects. Folic acid supplementation should begin 1 to 3 months prior to conception.</p><p>Prepregnancy visits allow a healthcare provider to optimize a patient's chances to have a successful pregnancy and healthy child. The purposes of these visits should be to identify risks to the mother or fetus and to intervene where possible to decrease these risks. Typical assessments include evaluating medical conditions (eg, obesity, diabetes mellitus, hypertension), social behaviors (eg, substance use), exposure to teratogens (eg, medications), genetic conditions or family history, and vaccination records. Laboratory evaluation commonly includes rubella and varicella serology to ensure immunity, as well as sexually transmitted infection screening in patients at high risk (eg, multiple sexual partners, no barrier protection). Finally, interventions include folic acid supplementation, abstinence from substance use, medication changes as needed, optimization of medical conditions, and required vaccinations. Folic acid supplementation in pregnancy is critical, as a deficiency can cause neural tube defects such as spina bifida; it is recommended for all patients planning on becoming pregnant or capable of becoming pregnant, as neural tube formation occurs early in preanancy, often before patients realize they are pregnant. The recommended dose for previously unaffected patients is 0.4 ma daily, starting at least 1 month prior to 1 to 3 months prior to conception.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and F. Genetic counseling (Choice A) may be useful during prepregnancy visits if the patient or their coparent has a personal or family history of inherited diseases, such as cystic fibrosis or sickle cell anemia. This patient and her partner are otherwise healthy, and while she has a strong family history of alcohol use disorder, this is not an inherited illness. Additionally, Guillain-Barré syndrome is acquired, rather than inherited. Weight loss (Choice B) is not necessary for this patient. While she is slightly overweight, she has no consequent health issues or obvious issues with ovulation. Once she becomes pregnant, she should be counseled that her expected appropriate weight gain would be less than a patient with a lower BMI. Chemical dependency evaluation (Choice C) is not recommended at this time. While this patient has a strong family history of alcohol use disorder, she has no personal history of substance use. Mammography (Choice D) is not warranted in this patient. Mammography is typically recommended in the setting of a palpable breast mass, which this patient does not have, or as a screening modality after 40 years of age. Removal of the verruca plantaris (Choice F) is not necessary prior to conception. While it may be offered, it will not affect this patient's ability to become pregnant nor her pregnancy itself.</p>", "45": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Vulvar carcinoma presents most commonly with a lesion of the labia majora in patients age 55 to 85 years. The lesion may be a papule, nodule, or plaque and may be ulcerated or friable. Other signs and symptoms include pruritus, bleeding, and inguinal lymphadenopathy. Diagnosis is made with a biopsy of the lesion, and treatment may include surgical excision, chemotherapy, and radiation depending on the extent of disease.</p><p>Vulvar carcinoma most commonly presents in older patients with a lesion on the labia majora that is often asymptomatic. The lesion may be a nodule, papule, or plaque, with varying degrees of ulceration. When present, other symptoms include pruritus or bleeding from the lesion. In addition to the lesion, physical examination may show inguinal lymphadenopathy, as seen in this patient, if the vulvar cancer has metastasized. The most frequent underlying histopathology is squamous cell carcinoma, and incidence is greatest in patients between the age 55 and 85 years. Risk factors include infection with human papillomavirus (HPV), cigarette smoking, immunosuppression, chronic inflammation (eg, lichen sclerosus), and a history of cervical cancer. Diagnosis is made with a biopsy of the lesion. Further evaluation should include cervical cancer screening, as concurrent cervical carcinoma is common, and abdominopelvic imaging to determine extent of disease. Treatment includes surgical excision and chemoradiation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, E, and F. h the vagina, vulva, penis, and/or anal canal. They do not typically cause inguinal lymphadenopathy or ulcerated lesions. Condylomata acuminata (Choice A) are associated with HPV, most commonly subtypes 6 and 11. They occur on the vulva, penis, and/or anal canal. They do not typically cause inguinal lymphadenopathy or ulceration. Herpes simplex (Choice B) genital infection is most commonly caused by herpesvirus 2 and is characterized by painful vesicles in the genital area (eg, vulva, vagina, cervix, perineum). Infection typically begins with the formation of vesicles, which lyse and progress to shallow, painful ulcers with an erythematous border. A single, pruritic, raised lesion would be unlikely. Primary syphilis (Choice C) presents with a single, nonpainful ulcer of the genitals referred to as a chancre. Primary syphilis can progress to secondary syphilis with a maculopapular rash and condyloma lata, and subsequently to tertiary syphilis with neurosyphilis and aortitis. While it may be associated with inguinal lymphadenopathy, the classic lesion of primary syphilis is generally nonpruritic. Vulvar dystrophy (Choice E) refers to chronic inflammatory changes of the vulva. This can be caused by lichen sclerosus, dermatitis, or chronic irritation. Presenting symptoms differ based on the underlying cause of the dystrophy but may include erythema, skin thickening, color changes, pruritic or painful lesions, and dyspareunia. A single, ulcerated lesion with associated inguinal lymphadenopathy is more likely caused by vulvar carcinoma than by vulvar dystrophy. Vulvar vestibulitis (Choice F) presents with erythema, inflammation, and hypersensitivity of the vestibule or opening of the vagina, leading to dyspareunia, particularly with entry. It would not be expected to cause a pruritic, raised lesion on the labia. Additionally, this patient has no history of dyspareunia.</p>", "46": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Normal puberty follows a course of thelarche (breast development), followed by pubarche (pubic hair growth) and a growth spurt, and finally by menarche (the start of menstruation) after age 8 years. If puberty occurs prior to this, it is referred to as precocious puberty, which can be further classified as central or peripheral.</p><p>Puberty in girls follows a predictable course of thelarche (breast development), followed by pubarche (pubic hair growth) and a growth spurt, and finally by menarche (the start of menstruation). Puberty is considered delayed in girls if breast development does not occur by age 12 to 13 years. This patient is 11 years old and has had a normal sequence of pubertal development with thelarche, pubarche, a growth spurt based on her being in the 75th percentile for height, and menarche. The most likely diagnosis is thus normal development.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D. Precocious puberty (Choice A) is the onset of pubertal development and secondary sexual characteristics before the age of 8 years in girls. It can be central or peripheral. Central precocious puberty is caused by activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis with increased follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinizing hormone (LH) concentrations.: (LH) concentrations. This early activation can be idiopathic or caused by central nervous system abnormalities, such as a brain tumor. Peripheral precocious puberty is caused by the release of excess sex hormones from the gonads or adrenal glands, or potentially the ectopic production of human chorionic gonadotropin from a germ cell tumor; FSH and LH concentrations are decreased. Premature adrenarche (Choice B) includes pubarche and the development of other secondary sexual characteristics, such as axillary hair, prior to age 8 years old in girls. This patient has only developed pubic and axillary hair during the last 6 months, which makes premature adrenarche incorrect. Premature menarche (Choice C) is uncommon. It refers to the development of menses without the appropriate secondary sexual characteristics. When present, it can be secondary to ovarian cysts or precocious puberty. However, this patient's menstruation followed the appropriate sequence of pubertal events. Premature thelarche (Choice D) often occurs before age 8 years in girls, and it may be the first presenting sign of precocious puberty. This patient's thelarche is not premature given her age of 11 years.</p>", "47": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Cervical cancer screening is recommended to be completed with cervical cytology via Pap smear every 3 to 5 years depending on the patient's age. If cervical cytology results show evidence of a high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion, the most appropriate next step in management is colposcopy and biopsy. High- grade lesions on histologic examination should undergo excision, although this is not recommended for pregnant patients in the absence of invasive disease given its risk for pregnancy complications such as cervical insufficiency and preterm birth.</p><p>Carcinoma of the cervix is classically associated with infection from human papillomavirus (HPV) high-risk strains 16, 18, 31, and 33. Risk factors include multiple sexual partners, smoking, and immunosuppression. Cervical cancer screening with Pap smear is recommended every 3 years for women age 21 to 29 years and every 5 years in conjunction with HPV cotesting (every 3 years if cotesting is not performed) for women age 30 to 65 years. The results of these screening tests then determine the need for further evaluation and diagnosis. The cervical cytology results may be normal, indeterminate, or show signs of atypical cells (atypical squamous cells of undetermined significance) or precancerous lesions (low- or high-grade intraepithelial lesions). High-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion, as seen in this patient, has an increased risk for progression to cervical cancer. Abnormal Pap smear results are evaluated by colposcopy, which allows for visual assessment and biopsy of abnormal lesions and is the most appropriate next step in management of this case. High-grade lesions on cervical cytology may be treated with excision via loop electrosurgical excision procedure or a cone biopsy of the cervix, but this is not recommended for a pregnant patient as it may lead to pregnancy complications such as cervical insufficiency and preterm labor.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E. Repeat Pap smear in 3 months (Choice A) is inappropriate. This patient has a high-grade lesion that is associated with current or future development of carcinoma in situ. She should undergo colposcopy and biopsy if warranted to avoid a delayed diagnosis of cervical carcinoma. Repeat Pap smear 6 weeks postpartum (Choice B) may be appropriate, but this would depend on the findings of the initial colposcopy and biopsy. If warranted to avoid a delayed diagnosis of cervical carcinoma, a patient with high-grade histology may have repeat examinations during pregnancy or shortly after delivery. Administer human papillomavirus vaccine (Choice C) is not warranted. Administration of HPV vaccination is recommended for patients up to age 26 years. In addition, this patient already has signs of HPV-induced cervical changes, making vaccination unlikely to be of benefit. Perform cryotherapy (Choice E) is incorrect. Cryotherapy is a treatment option for high-grade histology as confirmed by colposcopy with biopsy. It is appropriate for smaller lesions that do not extend into the cervical canal but is less commonly used than excisional procedures. This patient should have a histologic examination and diagnosis prior to proceeding to treatment.</p>", "48": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Symptomatic neonatal cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection can present with microcephaly. periventricular calcifications, chorioretinitis, jaundice, hepatosplenomegaly, thrombocytopenia, petechiae, seizures, and sensorineural hearing loss. Congenital CMV is the most common cause of nonhereditary sensorineural hearing loss and can present later in childhood in up to 10% of children who were asymptomatic at birth.</p><p>Congenital cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection is asymptomatic at birth in approximately 90% of newborns. When symptomatic in utero, ultrasonography may show hepatosplenomegaly, intrauterine growth restriction, microcephaly. or intracerebral (most often periventricular) calcifications. Fetal hydrops is a potential complication. Clinical features in symptomatic newborns include the above as well as transaminitis, jaundice, thrombocytopenia, petechiae, chorioretinitis, small size for gestational age, seizures, and sensorineural hearing loss. Long-term sequela in symptomatic infants, such as sensorineural hearing loss and neurodevelopmental abnormalities, are common. The risk for symptomatic neonatal infection is higher with a primary maternal infection than with recurrent infection. In adults, CMV is often asymptomatic or can cause a short, febrile, flu-like illness. Congenital CMV is the most common cause of nonhereditary sensorineural hearing loss and can present later in childhood in up to 10% of children who were asymptomatic at birth. Diagnosis of neonatal CMV can be confirmed with polymerase chain reaction testing or culture of urine or saliva. Treatment involves 6 months of oral valganciclovir and close, interdisciplinary follow-up.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. present with jaundice, hepatomegaly, and failure to thrive; central nervous system involvement is not consistent with hepatitis B. Hepatitis B virus (Choice B) is transmitted from mother to infant via contact with the mother’s blood and body fluids. Neonates are often asymptomatic at birth but can subsequently develop insidious-onset chronic liver disease. The microcephaly. intracranial calcifications, chorioretinitis, and sensorineural findings in this case are more consistent with congenital CMV. Parvovirus B19 (Choice C) can cause fetal anemia, which may present as fetal tachycardia, hydrops fetalis, high-output cardiac failure, and death. However, children who do not develop hydrops fetalis do not appear to suffer from long-term infectious sequelae. Central nervous system involvement and microcephaly are more consistent with CMV. Rubella virus (Choice D) infection in the newborn classically presents with a triad of cataracts, sensorineural deafness, and congenital heart disease. A bluish-red rash is often present. Intracerebral calcifications are not a feature of congenital rubella. Treponema pallidum (Choice E) can cause congenital syphilis via transmission through the placenta in the second and third trimesters. Manifestations include syphilitic rhinitis (eg, mucopurulent nasal discharge), rhagades, dental malformations (eg, notched teeth, irregularly shaped molars), and hepatomegaly with or without splenomegaly and lymphadenopathy. The time of presentation after birth is variable.</p>", "49": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Endometrial cancer can present with postmenopausal vaginal bleeding or abnormal uterine bleeding in premenopausal women. Risk factors for its development include prolonged, unopposed exposure to estrogen, such as early menarche, late menopause, chronic anovulatory cycles, exogenous estrogen therapy, and nulliparity. Diagnosis is made with endometrial biopsy, hysteroscopy, or dilatation and curettage.</p><p>Abnormal uterine bleeding should be suspected when bleeding is irregular, unpredictable, or not associated with usual premenstrual signs and symptoms (eg, bloating, breast fullness, uterine cramps). It may be caused by malignancy, structural abnormalities, such as cervical or endometrial polyps, adenomyosis, endometrial hyperplasia, inflammation or infection of the genital tract, coagulopathy, ovulatory dysfunction, endocrine dysfunction (eg, thyroid disorders, prolactin, androgens), and may be iatrogenic or idiopathic. In patients over age 45 years, who are perimenopausal or postmenopausal, there is a higher risk for endometrial hyperplasia and malignancy. Endometrial cancer commonly presents with postmenopausal vaginal bleeding, as seen in this patient. Risk factors include prolonged, unopposed exposure to estrogen, such as early menarche, late menopause, chronic anovulatory cycles (eg, polycystic ovary syndrome), exogenous estrogen therapy, nulliparity, and obesity. The most appropriate next step in evaluation of these patients is an endometrial biopsy to evaluate for the presence of endometrial cancer. If a patient cannot tolerate the procedure or if the procedure sampling is nondiagnostic, a dilatation and curettage or a hysteroscopy can be performed.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D. Measurement of serum estradiol concentration (Choice A) is not necessary in the evaluation of this patient's vaginal bleeding. Decreased concentrations of estrogen are associated with menopause and its classical vasomotor symptoms, vaginal atrophy, decreased bone density, and infertility. While both exogenous and endogenous sources of unopposed estrogen can lead to endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma, a measurement of estradiol concentration would neither confirm nor refute the diagnosis of endometrial carcinoma in this patient. Measurement of serum follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) concentration (Choice B) would likely be increased in this patient, as she is postmenopausal. FSH is responsible for ovarian stimulation early in the follicular phase. As ovarian estrogen production decreases in menopause as a result of ovarian follicle depletion, the negative feedback on FSH decreases, and its concentration increases. However, this measurement would not elucidate the reason for this patient's postmenopausal vaginal bleeding. Toxicology screening (Choice C) is not necessary in the diagnosis of postmenopausal bleeding. While cocaine or amphetamine use may be associated with vaginal bleeding during pregnancy secondary to placental abruption, these substances and other toxins do not routinely cause vaginal spotting in a postmenopausal patient. Progesterone challenge test (Choice D) is used in the evaluation of amenorrhea. A short course of progesterone is prescribed, and the patient is observed for uterine bleeding after its withdrawal. If bleeding occurs, the patient has adequate concentrations of estrogen to create an endometrial lining. If it does not, then the patient likely has hypoestrogenism or a structural defect of the uterus. This patient has postmenopausal vaginal bleeding rather than amenorrhea.</p>", "50": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Postpartum endometritis is a polymicrobial infection that presents with fever, uterine tenderness, and mucopurulent vaginal discharge. Risk factors include prolonged rupture of membranes, prolonged labor, cesarean delivery, bacterial vaginosis, group B Streptococcus colonization, and manual removal of the placenta. Treatment is with intravenous antibiotics.</p><p>Arecent postpartum patient with fever and uterine tenderness raises suspicion for endometritis, especially in the setting of prolonged rupture of membranes (time from rupture of membranes to delivery). Postpartum endometritis is an acute, typically polymicrobial infection of the uterine endometrium involving a mixture of aerobes and anaerobes from the genital tract. During labor and delivery, microbes from the genital tract can enter the uterine cavity and result in postpartum infection. Patients typically present with fever, tenderness of the uterine fundus, and mucopurulent vaginal discharge. Risk factors for endometritis include premature or prolonged rupture of membranes, prolonged labor, cesarean delivery, bacterial vaginosis, group B Streptococcus colonization, and manual removal of the placenta. Treatment requires broad- spectrum antibiotics such as clindamycin and gentamicin for appropriate antimicrobial coverage.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and F. Age of the patient (Choice A) is not a risk factor for the development of endometritis. Young maternal age does not independently increase postpartum infection risk. Duration of cesarean delivery (Choice B) is not associated with the risk for development of endometritis. Cesarean delivery itself, regardless of duration, does increase the tisk for endometritis, however. This is especially true for patients with bacterial vaginosis. Genetic predisposition (Choice C) is incorrect. There is no known genetic predisposition to endometritis. Exposure to general anesthesia (Choice D) would not increase the risk for endometritis in this patient. While neuraxial anesthesia is generally considered preferential for pregnant patients, general anesthesia does not increase the risk for infection in the postpartum period. Use of antibiotic prophylaxis (Choice F) would decrease, rather than increase, the risk for postpartum endometritis following cesarean delivery.</p>"};
